On 31 Jul 1996, Andy Newman wrote:

>   a concept, process some transactions, .... It's all people talking to
>   each other or making it possible to communicate an idea (e.g., "I want
>   to purchase your product" gets communicated via transaction processing).
>   It may be   difficult to stretch this to everything we do with these machines
>   but in many cases that's all we use them for - super typewriters - and the
>   rise of the 'net is just an extension of this - allowing people to
>   communicate more effectively. After all if you get a bunch of people
>   together what do they do? Communicate.
> 
> BTW, I have never owned a machine without a modem or some form of external
> communications ability.

Neither have I yet I still have MOST aspects of my PC that are simply not 
available, at all, to anyone else.  I also have software that is utterly 
independent of anyone or anything else.  I like it that way.  I dislike 
the idea of a communal storage medium that requires modem access, or some 
more expensive comm system, to get to it.  I simply do not want my 
computer to be, essentially, a smart telephone or cable box.  For some 
things and in some circumstances this may be a nice alternative *_or_ 
_something_in_addition_to_my_real_PC*.  

As for constant upgrades...I don't do those unless there is some added 
capability I want/need or modification to the way the software works that 
I prefer over the older version.  

I use OS/2 Warp.  My word processor is a great one called Describe 5.0.  
Describe went out of business last year (due to poor business practice, 
blah, blah, blah...) so there will be no upgrades to my Describe 
wordprocessor.  So freakin what?  It works great as I need it so I don't 
NEED to upgrade to anything else.  The same holds true for many other 
bits of software I have.  Hell, I have a piece of molecular biology 
software that is 3 or 4 years old and is DOS-based.  It can still do all 
the work I need it to so I do not need to upgrade it.  Thus, for me and I 
suspect for a good number of others, an NC doesn't offer any improvement, 
but rather, a reduction in capability.  I don't store my mail in a 
communal mailbox, I don't store my tax records in a communal filing 
cabinet, I don't store my official correspondence in a communal cabinet.  
I have my own text books, instruction manuals, games, music, etc, etc.  
They belong with me totally under my supervision and mine alone.  It 
doesn't all belong on some communal system that requires good phonelines, 
good administration, good devices, etc.  I can access computer files 
regardless of whether or not the school network goes down (ALL networks 
crap out now and again and any network-dependent computer would crap out 
too regardless of whether it was in perfect working order.  Ah, the 
network is down for an upgrade or has simply crapped out, I guess there 
wont be any computing TODAY...and possibly tomorrow either folks.  Since 
your personal storage and software has been removed you can't do shit but 
twiddle your thumbs).

NCs for certain business and academic and even SOME home computer 
uses, certainly, but hardly a "better" setup to my personal computer with 
personal software, records, devices, etc.  I can get work done regardless 
of line noise, network crapouts, hacking damage, etc.  You simply cannot 
even hack into my computer because my communication setup is outward.  
You cannot logon to my personal computer, nor can you call in and 
connect with my computer.  A hacker CAN dick up a network.  An angry 
employee can screw a network from the inside (it happened at the Univ of 
Utah last year)...or is the NC somehow perfect and to be free from 
bandwidth crunch, network glitches, hackers, and other interruptions?  Oh 
yeah, THAT will happen.

I called into my university earlier this evening to transfer a few 
files.  Lo and behold, everything happened in slooooooooooow 
mooooooootion because the network was loaded.  SAME thing would happen 
with consistent regularity with NCs.  You would end up having to do your 
real work at shitty-late hours when most people are in bed because the 
network is bogged down at the choice hours.  This problem is true with 
traffic arteries, plane flights, trains, movie theatres, amusement parks, 
restaurants, and networks.  There are times when it just plain sucks 
trying to do what you want or need done because everyone else is doin' it 
too.  At the same time.  The NC fantasy envisioned by Oracle duffus' is 
just that, a fantasy.  Just like any futurist's dream.  Reality ALWAYS 
brings the vision down to size.

patrick



