soup@kd3bj.ampr.org writes:
>There is still a serious amount of setup needed for an NC.  Somehow
>the fantasy that it'll work straight of the box bothers me.  *SOME*
>initial setup (such as acquiring the appropriate network connection,
>be it Modem, ISDN, ADSL, HDSL, cable modem or even EtherNet) is gonna
>be required.  Beyond that, setting it up w/ an IP address (or some
>facsimile thereof) is yet *another* headache.

There is setup required for a telephone connection. Even though you
can do it by twirling a few wires together, which is far easier than settng
the clock on most VCRs, we get people in to ensure its done correctly and
regulation exists to ensure that this most important machine (the inter-
-national telephone service) operates. When will the 'net reach this level
of importance? And when will the devices we hang off the 'net be as easy
to use as a telephone, if ever? Will everyone want a machine running one
of the several MS Windows products? Probably not.

Too many people seem to think that the current model of personal computing
(UI, apps, etc... ) that we have is the limit. Just because somebody tells
the press that the machine won't have any disk they just accept that. What
should have been said is that "users won't have to manage storage because
we're making a computing/communications* device that doesn't require them to."

* I don't think there needs to be any distinction between computing and
  communications. I believe that most computing is just a mechanism for
  more effective communications, e.g., crunch some numbers so they're
  more appropriate or easier to interpret, draw a picture to visualise
  a concept, process some transactions, .... It's all people talking to
  each other or making it possible to communicate an idea (e.g., "I want
  to purchase your product" gets communicated via transaction processing).
  It may be   difficult to stretch this to everything we do with these machines
  but in many cases that's all we use them for - super typewriters - and the
  rise of the 'net is just an extension of this - allowing people to
  communicate more effectively. After all if you get a bunch of people
  together what do they do? Communicate.

BTW, I have never owned a machine without a modem or some form of external
communications ability.
-- 
Andy Newman <andy@research.canon.com.au>



