Good observations. But I can top that.

Back in the batch IBM days of the early '70s, there were a bunch of
pinhead bureaucrats running the RPI computer center. In order to
encourage users to properly estimate their memory needs, there was a
charge for UNUSED MEMORY. Eg, if you specified in your job control
that you would need 512K bytes, but only used half that amount, you
were charged for the other half for as long as your job ran. At least
initially, this charge was outrageously high. It could dwarf all the
other charges, including CPU time, lines printed, pages printed, etc.
To the best of my recollection, they didn't actually charge for used
memory, only unused. So, if you were writing in assembler, you had to
make sure you allocated all the memory you could. You could then not
use it and not pay extra.

And some people STILL think that network computers will in some way
replace PCs. The more you give bureaucrats control over ANY aspect of
your computing environment, the more they will find ways to screw you.

