[John R. Campbell]

> There is still a serious amount of setup needed for an NC.  Somehow
> the fantasy that it'll work straight of the box bothers me.  *SOME*
> initial setup (such as acquiring the appropriate network connection,
> be it Modem, ISDN, ADSL, HDSL, cable modem or even EtherNet) is gonna
> be required.  Beyond that, setting it up w/ an IP address (or some
> facsimile thereof) is yet *another* headache.

Look at it this way: you buy the NC (oh, shucks, let's call it by its
real name: the terminal) that best suits your budget, and you plug it
into an outlet in your home that connects to the service provider
you've chosen to patronize, and you turn it on.  Voila -- up comes a
menu from the provider, complete with advertisements, free information
services and a login option for accessing the pay-as-you-go stuff.

Unplug the terminal, carry it into your neighbor's house, and plug it
into _their_ outlet, and if they subscribe to another provider, you'll
see a whole different setup.  In any case, the terminal has no setup
at all, it's _true_ plug'n'play.

Have your employer enable your home address for access, and you'll be
able to use the terminal for telecommuting -- when you choose "go to
work" from the menu, it'll look just like the one on you use there.

> As a side note, due to NT's evolution from DEC's VMS architecture,
> NT has inherited extremely powerful and tunable security mechanisms.
> They're also pretty damn subtle.  Who's gonna manage these machines
> when they reach the desktop?

NT is just another temporary thing on the mass market.  Sooner or
later, this moronic infatuation with the sort of products Microsoft
pushes will stop, and the focus will shift from OS and applications to
information access.  That's when the "NC" terminal will really
flourish in the private sector.  Those of us who actually _care_ about
computers and their software systems will go on using interesting and
powerful systems that we understand and enjoy, like UNIX or Win/NT.
People who only want access to information and entertainment will be
able to stop trying to understand operating system concepts.

> Sorry-  I'm a Linux/FreeBSD enthusiast...			:-)

Me too!  That is, I run NetBSD, because I need something that can run
on different hardware platforms.  If and when some sort of NC enters
my home, it won't be as a "computer", but as an access terminal for
information services that are distributed that way.  I'll continue to
run UNIX on my VAXen and PDPs and anything else I can get for free.
(I don't own a single computer that's worth _anything_ on the second
hand market, and I'm running state of the art UNIX and enjoying myself
tremendously, thank you!)

-tih



