After aquiring my own home internet account(s) my quarterly phone 
bill rose from 70 to 230 pounds sterling.  I also have a friend who
house and PC shares with two other people.  Their usage is less than
mine, but collectively their phone bill jumped by a similar
amount.       
Add to this the per hour charges from companies like AOL and you 
can double the total cost.    

If the UK follows the US and we get free local access, then that
will change the picture quite a bit. At the moment this is only 
being offered by one or two Mobile Phone companies and some Cable 
companies, even then, only during off peak times. So we've a 
way to go yet.

 
> What services would be provided, and how much would people
> be willing to pay for those services?

I only mentioned companies like AOL because they already have 
the experience linking home systems with computing resource at
the other end of a phone line (ie: non-internet).  They're also
$$ rich companies and might be able to make the investment to 
kick start the NC in the home.  I can also see companies like 
this trying to offer a "complete" NC solution, rather than a
home NC user having their services scattered among multiple 
companies.  One-stop-shopping, good access times and bill 
payment in one place would make this attractive to the consumer. 
And that's what the home NC is about, consumer computing, not
a PC replacement for academic tech heads.
  
> I had an X terminal running back in 1988.  Market penetration peaked
> around 1991 or so, and many are still around and sold, but market
> share has declined.  I still think it is a good idea in some cases.
> I don't see how an "NC" is really any different from an X terminal.

Well, I've not seen an NC yet so I can't tell you.  In day to day
use it might not appear to be any different, except for the 
MultiMedia bolt on bits.  I wouldn't try to run an X-terminal over
a V34 modem link though, would you?  

You know, some of you lot are missing the point. Just because it
isn't an attractive option for you doesn't mean that it won't be
just what someone else is looking for.  

Live and let live.
 
