It IS a very good thing for many applications, and this is a very sophisticated
'terminal' (relative to a vt100) too. For people trying to run a business
service on a server, 'supporting' millions of PCs distributed about the 
universe is a total nightmare. We constantly see very naive PC users (e.g. most
PC users) who complain to us about any number of problems accessing a service,
and of course it is always OUR problem, even if the real problem is their PC
hardware, any number of other departments installing stuff on and otherwise
fucking with their PC (then you have to track that down!), they themselves
hosing the PC, sheer user incompetance, or whatever. Simplifying the 'client
server' paradigm so the client can't get screwed up is just a smart business
decision. 


