> privacy of MY home.  I never want to have to depend upon a network of
> power stations and wires in order to run my home lighting.  :-) :-)

Ha ha!

Looking at it a different way:

Here I sit using a quizzillion MIPS sparcstation - MY software is
sitting in a collection of boxes all around San Jose (a word processor
here, an HDL simulator there, who knows where the 1/2 million dollar
synthesis tool sits). I don't know (offhand) what version of these
tools I'm using, I don't know the hotline support number, everything
just works like magic. MY data files sit in various boxes also
distributed around the site (in some pretty BIG boxes this time). I
don't know who does the backups - although I know who  to e-mail for
a recovery, I don't know what make the disk drives are, or even what
size they are (just that they are big enough!), I DO know that if there's
a fire here (or earthquake) somebody will have the guts of my system back
up before it stops smouldering (or wobbling).

I like that setup!

When I go home it's horrid. I have all sorts of software that (seems to
be) in constant need of upgrade. I'm forever running into bugs but I
don't know if there's a fix available or whether the "beta" version that's
advertized on the net is just another load of rubbish. I'm never sure
whether ALL my key s/w and data is backed up properly & secure from
virus attack. I'm sick and tired of having to reconfigure this, that and
everything just to try and use a new little whizz-bang network applet.
In short it's a lot of work & it's a lot more work than I want to do
before I can have fun & entertainment with MY expensive toy.

I think there are a lot of people out there who want something that works
straight out of the box and keeps working and doesn't require a degree
in computer science to maintain. Furthermore, they want something
that does not become obselete in the time it takes to wait at the traffic
lights on the way home.

> 
> >A network computer?  Not for me.  Oracle is dreaming.

They are dreaming, so am I - right now the internet is nothing more than
a dream for most people.
> 
> Maybe not for you, but the point is the NC will be used a bit
> differently than the PC. Oracle was not counting on you (or more
> broadly, people who use their PC's for sophisticated processing); it's
> meant for a larger but less sophisticated audience, those for whom the
> PC is just a communications & entertainment tool.
> 

Bearing in mind that people do some pretty sophisticated computing in
network based environments like the one I described above, I can see
a client-server type architecture could be applicable to a lot of people.
I hope that they (and other people who attack this market) think
carefully about what NEEDS to be local and what NEEDS to be central,
where to draw the lines and how to manage the communications within
practical bandwidth limits.

Hugh.

