[jason philbrook]

| We just lost both of our VR262 (black&white) monitors
| which were attached to DECstation 3100's. We leave the CPUs
| running all the time and shut the monitors off at night. 
| One morning, one of the VR262 monitors didnt turn on - no
| smoke and no drama. 3 days later, the other did the same.
| 
| Has anyone else had to deal with this and decide to fix the
| monitor? We *hope* there is an easy fix?

I've so far repaired two VR262 monitors that quietly died the way you
describe.  In both cases, the cause was poor soldering.  If you take
the cover off the monitor, you'll find a board that's vertically
mounted to one side of the tube, that has a tunable inductance on it
which has rather thick metal "feet" protruding through the board and
soldered to it -- but poorly, probably because they were never heated
sufficiently in the automatic solder bath or whatever they used.
Resoldering these did the trick for my monitors.

Be careful in there!  There's dangerous high voltage in any monitor,
even after it's been turned off and disconnected!  Use your head!

-tih



