Obviously our provider's news feed is broken, but I got the info I
needed via e-mail, from public news servers and even by phone...

Thanks to

76703.501@compuserve.com (Stuart Fuller)
Andreas Grzybowski <grzybows@transtec.de>
BENNETT@MPGARS.DESY.DE
Brad Spencer <brad@chem.wisc.edu>
Monscheuer@t-online.de (Michael Monscheuer)
Scott Mc Fadden <mcfadden@iscg.com>
Stephan Christen <schriste@iiic.ethz.ch>
Tony Konashenok <tonyk@sseos.lbl.gov>
dcarson@protocom.com (Dan Carson)
hannon@physics.ucdavis.edu (Mike Hannon (UCD Physics, 916-752-4966))
hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoffman)
jgreen@nimr.mrc.ac.uk (John Green)
psmode@jeslacs.bc.ca (Peter A. Smode)
Vance Haemmerle <vance@alumni.caltech.edu>

and all others whose replies didn't get across to me.

The problem turned out to be an incompatibility between the 5.5-2H4
SCSI driver and the disk's firmware. Basically, three solutions were
offered:

1. modify the disk using some SCSI utility or have it modified by
   the vendor (our distributor offed to modify it for free)

2. patch the SCSI driver.

3. Update to a more recent version of VMS (impossible for us since
   we still need VAX P.S.I.)

We took the second approach and applied patch VAXSCSI03_061 (available
from ftp.service.digital.com in /pub/vms/vax/v5.5-2/). This solved the
problem.

Interestingly, the patch does not cure the problem on systems older
than 5.5-2H4. You have to upgrade to 5.5-2H4 first to make it work.

Thanks again
Steffen.

