I agree with the first  part, but I'm not so sure  about the
second.  Never mind the  technology,  a diskless  NC at home
which is  totally  reliant  on dial in  access  to a service
provider  is today, in the UK,  unafordable  for  most.  The
cost  of  the  telephone  (local  rates)  +  hourly  service
provider  charges  for say, 4 hours per day  (activity  from
husband + wife + kids) is beyond  the budget of most  family
households.  The cost  will  have to come  down.  Either  by
changing the method of  charging,  for example by the amount
of  data  transfered  instead  of  connection  time,  or  by
technology  advances  delivering  split second connect times
and unit  rates.  Either  that,  or home  NC's  come  with a
smallish  local  disk for  holding  local  data and  caching
frequently used applications.

I think these obstacles will eventually be overcome.  People
always  find a way when  there's  money to be made.  But the
home NC market will be slow  getting off the ground and will
probably be led by existing  companies like Compuserve, AOL,
et al making the  transition.  Corporate  Intranets  however
are an ideal  environment  and the NC  argument  there  is a
strong  one.  It's this  market  that will keep the NC alive
until the home market is ready for it.

