organization: Stanford Linear Accelerator Center
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <suzukis-2507961910290001@ppp097.inv.co.jp>, 
    			suzukis@inv.co.jp (Suzuki shigeki) writes:
> I have some difficuties to use DEC VAX-Alpha for ISSA thermal analysis
> using TRASYS and SINDA. TRASYS and SINDA for VAX is only available and for
> VAX-Alpha is not available. 
>  This difference of FORTRAN architecture between VAX and VAX-Alpha makes
> it difficult to port these codes to VAX-Alpha. 
> Why it is so difficult to port FORTRAN source code from VAX to VAX-Alpha ?

        First  some  terminology.   There   is   no   such  thing  as  a
    "VAX-Alpha".   There is VAX, and there is Alpha, and these refer  to
    two different  _hardware_  architectures.   In  addition,  there  is
    Openvms/VAX  and  OpenVMS/Alpha,  the  same  VMS  _operating system_
    running on the two different  architectures.   (It used to be called
    "VAX/VMS"  before  there  was Alpha, but they changed  the  name  to
    OpenVMS/VAX as of VMS V5.5-2.) And to be  complete,  there  is  also
    Digital  Unix  and  Windows/NT  that  runs  on  Alpha  (but not VAX)
    hardware.

        So, let's assume you've got  some  Fortran code that works under
    OVMS/VAX  but  not  under OVMS/Alpha.  Please let  us  know  _which_
    version of OVMS/VAX you're  running,  which  version  of  OVMS/Alpha
    you're  running  ($  SHOW  SYSTEM  will  tell you), and perhaps more
    importantly, which versions of Fortran you're running on the VAX and
    on the Alpha.  There are a  lot  of  changed in Digital Fortran V6.0
    and above (which is available on both VAX and Alpha) relative to VAX
    Fortran  V5.8 and below.  Many assumptions about static storage  and
    alignment under VAX Fortran don't hold under Digital Fortran.  Also,
    many "dirty programming" tricks that worked on the VAX hardware fail
    on the Alpha hardware.  For example, on VAX you could pass a  single
    precision  REAL  variable to a DOUBLE PRECISION actual argument, and
    except for the  least  significant  mantissa  bits,  this would work
    fine.   It fails under Alpha for a couple of reasons, one being that
    double precision uses G_float format for calaculations  rather  than
    D_float  as  on  the VAX.  Another being that the system detects and
    complains about  "dirty  zeros",  REALs  with  a  zero  exponent but
    non-zero mantissa.  On the VAX, only the exponent (and the sign bit)
    were checked.

        So in general, what makes  porting  "hard" is that you've got to
    make  your  code  more standard-conforming.  It helps  to  read  the
    various documents on VAX to Alpha porting.  There are  a  number  of
    them,  as  well  as  an  article  Steve Lionel wrote specifically on
    porting Fortran from VAX to Alpha.

        If you still have trouble,  you'll  need  to provide some of the
    specific  information requested above, and the exact errors you  get
    when you try to compile on Alpha.  Oh, but if TRASYS and  SINDA  are
    commercial products, there's no reason the vendor can't port them to
    Alpha.   Have  you  asked  them  to?   If  it's  reasonable standard
    Fortran, there shouldn't be any problems...

        -Ken
