In article <4t76s7$ug5@styx.uwa.edu.au>, 
mustang@tartarus.uwa.edu.au (David Manchester) writes:

> I am chasing almost any info on the MicroVAX3300 

> Firstly: Physical size is the main concern for freight...
> 	 what box are they in, a BA23, BA123 ??

The 3300 comes in a BA-215 "skunk box" cabinet.   This
is the small version of the BA-213.   The BA-215 only
has six Q-Bus slots.   The box is about 15" square and
27" high.   There is space for three RF3x disks and one
TK70 tape drive in the top portion of the cabinet.

> - more importantly, what do they weigh?

A quick weigh-in (umpf!) shows that a typical 3300 system
weighs about 75 pounds, without cables, etc.

> Would a 3300 have a SCSI controller in a stock machine, 

No, but the KA640 CPU board has a built-in DSSI disk controller,
and RF3x disks are usually available pretty cheap.   Most
3300 systems came with a DEC TK70 tape drive built in.

> or were they 3rd party? 

There are some 3rd party Q-Bus SCSI controllers, such as CMD
and (my favorite) Andromeda.

> And indeed would it just have an RQDXx MFM controller?

I have heard rumors that some were built like that, but
have never seen one.

> How does the KA640 compare to the uVAXII we have, CPU-performance-wise?

About 2.4 to 3 times the speed.

> Are they clusterable 

Absolutely (with VMS), using the Ethernet interface built into
the CPU board.

> and will they cluster under Ultrix-32?

I don't know anything about Ultrix, so I cannot answer this.

