Steffen Kluge wrote:
>
> (This message has been previously posted to comp.os.vms)
>
> Hi everyone in VMSland,
>
> I'm trying to add a Seagate SCSI disk (ST31051N, 1GByte) to an
> older MicroVAX 3100 (KA42A v1.3 console) running VMS 5.5-2H4.
>
> The drive is properly recognised and displayed with `show device'
> on the console prompt. Under VMS, however, an attempt to INIT the
> disk results in a fatal drive error message.
>
> The drive itself seems to be ok, it works flawlessly on a 200 4/233
> running VMS 6.2 and on a 3000/300 running DEC Unix.
>
> What can I do to make it run on the MicroVAX? Needless to say that I
> tried all reasonable jumper settings on the drive. BTW upgrading
> the OS is not a option because we would lose the VAX PSI
> functionality :-(
>

Patches, patches, patches...

From a DIA article:
-OpenVMS VAX V5.5-2H4 and V6.1 supports Tagged Command Queuing (TCQ)
-based on the SCSI-2 standard.  In order to find out if a device
-supports Tagged Command Queuing, OpenVMS validates information
-reported by the device about its capabilities.  SEAGATE drives report
-information different from what is expected by OpenVMS, and the device
-is not mounted.

This might not be the specific problem you are having, but it points out the
need for the VAXSCSI ECO kit mentioned in the article.

I remember that there was a big issue about the good ol' VAXSCSI ECO kit that
addressed a number of SCSI compatibility issues through the older VMS versions.
Obstensibly, VMS supported all of these devices; however, DEC is rather anal
about standards conformance, and their drivers have never been that tollerant
of slight deviations (eg. modem handling in the serial device drivers). With
SCSI though, they ended up patching in some tollerance to deal with a number of
thrid party (cheaper than DEC) drives that everybody ended up buying.

-- Peter
P.S. I have a number of old uVAXen running Seagate drives on similar VMS
versions. I *think* I even have one with that specific drive.
