Terry Donald  <donald@eng.pko.dec.com> wrote:
>
>The AlphaStation 200 systems originally had 1 MB of flashROM - enough to hold the ARC
>firmware for the Windows NT o/s and the SRM firmware for the OpenVMS and Digital UNIX o/s. 
>Later half of the flashROM was removed. This meant either ARC or SRM could be loaded, but 
>not both. Typing "verify" at the APU prompt tried to verify both, even though you have 
>only SRM, hence the error. Try typing "verify srm" to check just the SRM firmware image.
>
>--Terry 

Terry,

Two questions:

Assuming one has an earlier machine (with 1MB flashrom), in a dual boot
(Digital UNIX & NT) environment, how does one go about upgrading
both the ARC & SRM?  Is it as simple as loading the SRM update when you're
running the SRM console, switching over to ARC & loading the ARC update from 
that side?  Having a 'vrfy' error on the ARC firmware would make me a little
nervous to switch from SRM to ARC..

Another one for you: Is it possible to switch beteen the SRM & ARC firmware
in the later machines?  In one of our 200s, (with 1MB) we can currently just
say 'set ostype nt' (or something similar, I don't have my cheat sheet in front
of me) to switch to NT with no hassle, and we can use the ARC menus to get
back to UNIX.  What happens when you have a newer box running in ARC
console mode & tell it to switch to a Digital UNIX console. If there's no
SRM firmware there, what does it do???

One last one -- What happens if a firmware update gets botched?  (say the 
power goes out after the erase step), how can one recover the system?
This has never happened to me, but I'm always a little nervous when I 
do the upgrade, and I'm curious if there's an emergency recovery procedure.


Thanks for all the info,

Drew





