In article <4ta8lt$ctr@vidar.diku.dk>, torbenm@diku.dk (Torben AEgidius Mogensen) writes:
|> Oracle's NC is not (initially, at least) going to use Suns Java chips,
|> but ARM processors. The first models will probably use a ~33MHz
|> ARM7500, but already this year I expect models with 300MHz StrongARMs.
|> That should easily be enough to run Doom look-alikes. As for needing
|> disks, I don't think that is essential, especially if you have a
|> reasonable amount of RAM to cache textures.

This is kind of a tangent, but have you personally looked at any of these 
games recently?

The typical media used for distributing games these days is CD-ROM, and 
many of them completely fill at least one (and sometimes 2) disks. Yes,
that's right, 650-1300 MB for a lousy game. Probably a lot more once 
higher-density technologies become available. These suckers make MS-Office 
look like a model of compactness. 

Shove *that* down your cable modem and smoke it. 

More seriously, if home computing devices needs to support this kind of
data hunger, then maybe NC-style centralization simply isn't the most 
economical way of doing things. The deciding factor will be how the cost 
of putting mass-storage devices in the clients compares to the incremental 
infrastructural cost to provide bandwidth sufficient to relace said
mass-storage devices.

A lot of it depends on what kind of application you choose to look at.
It looks to me as though games will be among the more difficult
applications to implement economically on NC's. Unfortunate, since games 
presently move a lot of computing devices off of retail shelves and into
the home.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
