In <Pine.SOL.3.91.960723084934.29632B-100000@howard> Patrick <patrick@howard.genetics.utah.edu> writes:

>Every time I hear this nonsense about Network Computers as a replacement 
>for the PC I cringe and feel violent.  I will NEVER accept such a toy.  I 
>want MY PC to hold MY software and MY data files right there in the 
>privacy of MY home.  I never want to have to depend upon a network 
>storage device or the internet in order to run my computer.  I want my 
>system to be connected yet independent of all others.  I want to be able 
>to run what I want when I want regardless of whether or not phonelines 
>are interrupted, the internet traffic is heavy and slowing things down 
>(happens often enough as it is).  I will never trust my personal data to 
>a communal storage source.  I like having everything on hand.

Very well said.

IMO the biggest strength of a PC is that it's a PERSONAL computer.
Not dependent on MIS for basic operation. Not dependent on fat and
lazy telephone monopolies or incompetent cable bureaucracies.
Doesn't require a huge monthly cash outflow to remain useful.

There are still plenty of us who remember the tyranny of the mainframe
back in the '70s. Nothing like having a project due at the end of a
semester and having to wait two days for the mainframe to find a syntax
error in your card deck. Nothing like having maybe $30 of "funny money"
for computer time for a semester course and making sure that you could
complete all the assignments without running out.

Those of us who survived mainframes will NEVER give up the independence
of PERSONAL computing. Almost as important as personal firearms. :-)

>... [more good stuff deleted] ...

>A network computer?  Not for me.  Oracle is dreaming.

Oracle and all the other fools chasing the network computer market
are in for a great deal of disappointment.

There will probably be some fascist MIS departments that succeed
in deploying these to some small extent in corporate "intranets".
But it will be a relatively small niche. And if it doesn't achieve
critical mass it will fade away and disappear. Much more likely is
that the brutal price competition in full-blown PCs will ensure that
they remain the much smarter choice.

There are others who suggest that, for the home environment, the game
companies will be more successful than the Oracle's of the world.
For example, the Sega Channel is enjoying some popularity in allowing
people to download games from the "network". But this is clearly an
addition to, not a replacement for, standalone operation.

No way that Larry Ellison will outsmart Bill Gates. Bill has proven
himself to be a very smart businessman and a 20-year industry survivor.
And, amusingly enough, on the PBS show Ellison came across as an even
more pompous and conceited schmuck than I've ever seen Gates to be.
So I'm sure not too many people will cry for Larry after he crashes
with this dumb scheme.

-- 
