organization: Design Technology, Intel Corporation
newsgroups: comp.sys.intel,comp.sys.dec,comp.arch


In article <4t2jc0$jpr@seva.mdx.ac.uk> david20@alpha1.mdx.ac.uk (D.Webb) writes:

> From: david20@alpha1.mdx.ac.uk (D.Webb)
> Newsgroups: comp.sys.intel,comp.sys.dec,comp.arch
> Date: 23 Jul 1996 13:16:48 GMT
> Organization: Middlesex University, London, England
> Reply-To: david20@alpha1.mdx.ac.uk
> 
> In article <4t0bpv$ido@dailyplanet.wam.umd.edu>, veakblad@Glue.umd.edu (David T. Wang) writes:
> >
> 
> Can someone please tell me why they think the network bandwidth needed for
> NCs is going to be so terrible on an intranet. OK there are bandwidth problems

	NC like infrastructure for the intranet should be a viable 
implementation.  But the problem is the logistics of the models proposed
by Larry Ellison.  The truth is NC like client within a corporate
environment already exists today as "Application Specific Computing
Appliance" as proposed by companies like ACER. (BTW, it is available
today)  The idea is a stripped down version of PC with enhanced
networking capability priced at  between $500-$1000, that hooks up to a 
server cluster.  This is very reminecient of the old dumb terminal
hooking up to time sharing monolithic IBM mainframes.  The difference
of course is that the dump terminal has its own local computation power
for running client applications, but the data essentially is kept in the
server clusters.  

> if you want to do this over the internet - but then you are talking about 
> overloaded international links etc.

	Essentially, the governments will have to over see the "links"
and regulated as public utilty since no private company has the means
to maintain the national network.

> On a companies intranet however you are already using more bandwidth than the
> NCs will take with the current generation of Networked PCs. The current
> situation is not a PC with all its files and programs stored locally. It is a
> number of networked systems connected to a fileserver running NETWARE or some
> other Network operating system. Each PC downloading Wordperfect etc and writing
> user files to disks on the server. That is what takes up a lot of bandwidth.
> I can't see that using a NC will make this situation worse it may even make it
> better since the NC will be designed from the start with this in mind whereas
> this mode of operation is something the PC was never designed for.
> 
> One of the biggest problems with managing PCs is the fact that incompatible
> addons - new graphics cards etc are constantly being produced. This means that
> it is difficult to provide a centralised setup on a server which can be
> downloaded and run properly on all PCs.
> The NC will not provide this problem since such functions will probably be in
> the firmware and applications will be downloaded in a device independent format
> ie HTML and JAVA.  

	The problem of course is if all the NC manufcturers can agree
to one single standard in order to make network management simple.
One way to achieve connectivity of course is to buy all the networked
computers from a single source.  If so, why not just buy all the PCs
from a company like HP and have then configure everything for you?

- Chung


