davidsen@tmr.com (bill davidsen) wrote:
[snip]
>The markup is higher on handmade parts, so I guess the markup on
>very low volume parts would be pretty high.

    Those DEC elves must have steady hands to carve those 0.25 um
    effective gate lengths; I'm impressed, my hands are too shakey
    to solder 20 mil pitch surface mount packages. 
>
>|                                 The first day sales of the 64 bit MIPS
>|    based Nintendo 64 video game in Japan was 300,000 machines and they
>|    expect to better that in the U.S.  These sales represent consumer
>|    dollars (or Yen) that might have otherwise gone to low end PC sales.
>
>I took this out of context, since it's pretty unrelated to the rest
>of the paragraph. I don't see how you can draw this conclusion,
>other than it is true that if I buy a new fishing rod, or trap gun,
>or set of golf clubs, that is money I could have used to buy a PC. I
>don't seriously contemplate using a PC for catching fish, breaking
>clay birds, or hitting a golf ball, so they are not directly
>competing for the same dollars.

   I hate to tell you this but nearly half of PCs sold in the U.S.
   go into the home.  Of these, a large number are bought to play
   games.  AFAIK, 16 bit stereo sound and 6X CD drives aren't needed
   to run MS Office.

>
>|    The network computer represents a similar threat to Intel and MS as
>|    the potential global volumes could eclipse the total PC market (the
>|    businesses and consumers in second and third world countries can't
>|    afford to place and maintain Pentium PCs on everyone's desk) 
>
>And of course these countries have a network infrastructure already
>in place? This is totally unrealistic. Note that while a free

    Some of these countries are bypassing large investments in
    copper subscriber loops and going directly to cellular and 
    wireless solutions.  Given the skewed income distribution,
    this allows infrastructure (albeit sparse) to be built
    sooner than might otherwise be economically justified.

>standaing PC can be used with zero technical support people standing
>by, a network does *not* work that way. Not only is there no
>network, there is no money for a network, and no people to run a
>network.
>
[snip]
>|                                                          If you need
>|    a generic "125 MIPs" of integer performance what are you going to
>|    pick for your incredibly cost sensitive, possibly battery powered
>|    product? a ~$35 1/2 W StrongARM, a ~$35 1.8 W 64 bit MIPS R4300i,
>|    or a ~$150 10+ W (worst case) Pentium P54/100 (which also requires
>|    a wider and faster bus)? ROTFLMAO!
>
>I think the need for generic MIPS is yet to be proven, and the idea
>that Intel makes only x86 is popular but incorrect. I believe the
>860/960 RISC products were the largest selling 32 bit CPUs for most
>of a decade, and may still be since I don't see the numbers much
>anymore.

    Maybe you should talk to MIPS about who is buying their chips.
    Seeing as they expect to sell about 10 million processors this
    year, and SGI and NEC workstations and servers probably account
    for less than 100,000 of them, someone sure seems to have a need
    for generic 32/64 bit mips.  Add to that millions of ARMs, 960s,
    68K/683XX, embedded PPCs, Hitachi SHs and so on.

    Compared to some RISCs targeted at the embedded market, like the
    SA-110 and R4300i, the 960 is looking mighty tired these days.
    Even newer 960 parts are clearly outclassed in price, performance,
    and power dissipation.  The problem with the 960 line is that
    Intel's fab capacity in its 0.35 um and 0.6 um processes is tied
    up with x86 production.  These wafers bring in much more money and
    profit when stepped with Pentiums rather than shrunk 960 dice.

>
>|                                                                 All
>|    the while, the x86 cloners will be forcing Pentium margins down
>|    and increasingly desperate PC makers start looking for alternatives
>|    to the Intel hegemony.
>
>Is this a dream you had, or what? The P5 price cuts, before
>competition, forced the margins down on 486 clones, to the point

     I think you mean P54C rather than P5.  The P5 (original 0.8 um,
     5V Pentium) was a very costly part to make given its 306 mm2
     die size, and wasn't particularly faster than a high end 486 on
     unscheduled code.  The shrunk 486 (81 mm2) was a much more
     profitable use of Intel's 0.8 um wafer capacity than the P5.
     The Pentium didn't really become *practical* until 0.6 um (P54C),
     and hugely profitable until 0.35 um (P54CS).

     Why don't you graph the price of Intel x86 processors against
     time and note where competition arrived at each processor
     class.  I think you might see some interesting patterns
     emerge.

>that they are barely worth making. The P6 has less effect because it
>is much more of a 32 bit chip than previous Intel chips, and the
>failure of Win95 and NT to move the market quickly has resulted in
>less demand for a 32 bit (mostly) chip. I won't speculate on the P7,
>because I know little and what I do know is probably semi
>confidential.
>
>I think if you expect Intel to suddenly lose market share, you are
>in for a disapointment. They have the R&D, the plants and the
>processes to make whatever the market wants, the resources to delay
>the decisions without playing catchup (see my sig), and the money
>and ruthlessness to influence market direction.

    I don't see things so black and white.  As the bulk of the
    business market moves over to Win NT and 486 class machines
    are discarded, I would expect Intel's market share of this
    segment will actually increase.  The Alpha will steal share
    from the mid to high end of the Win NT market, particularly
    as more high performance applications appear.  The x86 cloners
    don't have a good story yet for NT and will likely lose share
    regardless of how fast Alpha catches on.

>	Bill Davidsen (davidsen@tmr.com)
>"The secret to procrastination is to put things off until the
> last possible moment - but no longer"  -me

Paul

All opinions strictly my own.

--
 Paul W. DeMone       The 801 experiment SPARCed an ARMs race to put
 Kanata, Ontario      more PRECISION and POWER into architectures with
 pdemone@tundra.com   MIPSed results but ALPHA's well that ends well.

Satan's CPU? The P666 of course - check out Appendix H(ell) under NDA




