>competition, forced the margins down on 486 clones, to the point

     I think you mean P54C rather than P5.  The P5 (original 0.8 um,
     5V Pentium) was a very costly part to make given its 306 mm2
     die size, and wasn't particularly faster than a high end 486 on
     unscheduled code.  The shrunk 486 (81 mm2) was a much more
     profitable use of Intel's 0.8 um wafer capacity than the P5.
     The Pentium didn't really become *practical* until 0.6 um (P54C),
     and hugely profitable until 0.35 um (P54CS).

     Why don't you graph the price of Intel x86 processors against
     time and note where competition arrived at each processor
     class.  I think you might see some interesting patterns
     emerge.

>that they are barely worth making. The P6 has less effect because it
>is much more of a 32 bit chip than previous Intel chips, and the
>failure of Win95 and NT to move the market quickly has resulted in
>less demand for a 32 bit (mostly) chip. I won't speculate on the P7,
>because I know little and what I do know is probably semi
>confidential.
>
>I think if you expect Intel to suddenly lose market share, you are
>in for a disapointment. They have the R&D, the plants and the
>processes to make whatever the market wants, the resources to delay
>the decisions without playing catchup (see my sig), and the money
>and ruthlessness to influence market direction.

    I don't see things so black and white.  As the bulk of the
    business market moves over to Win NT and 486 class machines
    are discarded, I would expect Intel's market share of this
    segment will actually increase.  The Alpha will steal share
    from the mid to high end of the Win NT market, particularly
    as more high performance applications appear.  The x86 cloners
    don't have a good story yet for NT and will likely lose share
    regardless of how fast Alpha catches on.

