>To make software fast enough to be useful over a net, you need about
>10mbit to the client. I've used 4mbit token ring, and T1 feeds, and
>they range from lesiurely to painful. It's not clear that this
>bandwidth is available in a useful form right now.

It is easily done in a business environment.  We currently buy 24 port
ethernet switches for a little over 2000 pounds, each switch also has 
2 fast ports, usually 100 Mbit/s Fast ethernet (this can also be Full
Duplex 100 Mbit).  Connect these with 100 Mbit switches and you have 
a network which can handle just about anything.  Once each machine has
a camera and is sending out live video feeds maybe we'll need to upgrade
to 100 Mbit to the desk and 1 Gbit between the switches but that is a 
few years down the line.

