On Wed, 24 Jul 1996, Phillip Fayers wrote:

> In article <Dv0GyB.AD7@fasttech.com>, zeke@fasttech.com (Bohdan Tashchuk) writes:
> >In <Pine.SOL.3.91.960723084934.29632B-100000@howard> Patrick <patrick@howard.genetics.utah.edu> writes:
> >
> >>Every time I hear this nonsense about Network Computers as a replacement 
> >>for the PC I cringe and feel violent.  I will NEVER accept such a toy.  I 
> >>want MY PC to hold MY software and MY data files right there in the 
> >>privacy of MY home. 
[...]
> When I hear people talking about NCs as replacements for PCs I also cringe,
> but I fully support the NC.  Having the job of managing about 60 machines I
> can see all the advantages!
[...]
> >IMO the biggest strength of a PC is that it's a PERSONAL computer.
> >Not dependent on MIS for basic operation. Not dependent on fat and
> >lazy telephone monopolies or incompetent cable bureaucracies.
> >Doesn't require a huge monthly cash outflow to remain useful.
[...]
> The biggest problem at work with managing PCs is that they are personal,
> put one on someones desk and within a few weeks the setup is totally
> different to any other PC in the building.
[...]

My problem with the idea of a NC is not that they might come to exist, 
but that there is some idea that they will replace PCs.  Ptooie! 

Not a chance.  An NC might well make sense in some business and academic
environments:  I could see an advantage, so long as the bandwidth is great
enough to support everyone without appreciable slow-down, to have our
various departments having their own common network hub to which a bunch
of dept NCs attach.  The Biochemistry Dept, etc, might make out OK with
such a system at the University.  Or the Institute of Human Genetics and
other entities here could use such a local network storing for use common 
programs.  However, our network as is gets slow during times of heavy 
use...and there is no reason to expect that this would change with NCs.  
As a matter of fact, since an NC would absolutely require the net for 
functionality, traffic would instantly reach 100% for the number of boxes 
used and would lead to slow computing.  

Hell, just logging onto my university's network so I could get into the 
newsgroups was slow as shit this afternoon due to high traffic.  Every 
day everyone would experience this same situation here is the computers 
did not have their own individual storage systems so that they could 
operate independently of all others.  And what of specialty software?  
The lab I am attached to makes use of some software that no other here do 
(and vice versa).  It is because we have our own storage media that we 
can use this software...otherwise, we would have to justify being, 
perhaps, the only lab here using a bit of software that would now have to 
be purchased by the net administrators for the common storage site.  
So...you let a bunch of administrators and beaurocrats determine what 
software is available to you to use based on their rules and ideas rather 
than the requirements of OUR lab.  Not acceptable.

Where NC goes wrong big time is in the idea that I and many others would
select a NC for major home use in replacement of our present PCs.  THAT is
where my hackles raise.  I cannot see it as anything more than a snazzy 
cable box.  Whoopee.

I wont go through the list again but it is important to me and, 
apparently, many others that we keep our PERSONAL computer with all its 
abilities.  I will never give up my personal storage or personal files 
and software.  They be MINE and MINE alone for use whenever and however I 
feel like it...and that is enough to kill the NC in my own eyes.

patrick





