Good points, Paul.

Writing from Singapore, I can tell you the whole of East Asia, 
especially mainland China, is leapfrogging the whole copper base and 
going after fiber optic and wireless straight - at least in major cities 
over 1 million (just those combined make for one whole USA) and actual 
per capita income in those cities would allow network computers for most
families there. In Singapore, every housing block has a fiber optic, and 
in 1998 the whole city-state will be cable modem-enabled. Even with 
bandwidth sharing, that still means 1 Mbps to every home. I am personally 
not a NC proponent, but anything that can challenge the real monopolist, 
Intel (not Microsoft), is welcome.

BTW, if there is one architecture that can challenge Intel in high-end PC 
'power desktop' market, that is Alpha:
- compelling performance advantage
- good bunch of NT software
- standard hardware environment
- drastically dropping prices close to Pentium Pro levels

And now, even marketing flops (often seen) by DEC can't stop it, as other 
big names like Samsung and Mitsubishi enter the game, providing the 
ideal three CPU sources, excellent for system vendors. See even more for 
yourself on October 22 when 21264 announcement comes...

