In article <4scuhn$glp@csl2.csl-gmbh.net>, kluge@ave.ac.agit.de
(Steffen Kluge) wrote:

>(This message has been previously posted to comp.os.vms)

>Hi everyone in VMSland,

>I'm trying to add a Seagate SCSI disk (ST31051N, 1GByte) to an
>older MicroVAX 3100 (KA42A v1.3 console) running VMS 5.5-2H4.

>The drive is properly recognised and displayed with `show device'
>on the console prompt. Under VMS, however, an attempt to INIT the
>disk results in a fatal drive error message.

>The drive itself seems to be ok, it works flawlessly on a 200 4/233
>running VMS 6.2 and on a 3000/300 running DEC Unix.

>What can I do to make it run on the MicroVAX? Needless to say that I
>tried all reasonable jumper settings on the drive. BTW upgrading
>the OS is not a option because we would lose the VAX PSI
>functionality :-(

Whenever you get  a "fatal drive error" or "fatal controller error",
you should go immediately to the error log (ANALYZE/ERROR_LOG) to see
just what the error is.  Otherwise, you're just guessing and are
wasting your time.

That said, the problem is most likely that the drive has some internal
error handling bits turned on that the SCSI disk driver doesn't like.
It works fine with Digital Unix and with VMS V6.2, since Unix doesn't
care, and VMS V6.1 and later doesn't care, either.

The bits are labeled ARWE and ARRE (Automatic Reassign [block] on
Write/Read Error). 

Solution?  I've read that there are patches available for the SCSI
disk driver that turn off the check for these bits.  Or, you can
return the drive to your dealer to see if he can turn the bits off.
Upgrade to a more recent version of VMS that doesn't check the bits.





