>
>	My company is currently looking at moving over to a 
>Client/Server environment and we want to know if the newest version of 
>DECforms would be the way to go.  Supposedly it will work on a 
>windows-based machine.  Are there any other ways to talk to a VAX/VMS 
>environment from a windows-based machine???

I like the DECforms runtime environment, but IFDL, the source language for the
forms, is not anything that should have to be written by a programmer.  There
is an interactive forms development utility, but it's pretty limited, so you
have to write IFDL source code yourself.  Thousands of lines of it.

If you have terminals running ACMS DECforms applications and want to add PCs to
the configuration, I would recommend their new support for Visual Basic.  For
new applications, I wouldn't even think about an old character cell forms
system that had bitmap display support grafted onto it and requires thousands
of lines of source code for all but the most trivial forms.

Unless you get paid by number of lines of source code generated, look for a
real Windows client generator and a way for it to talk to the server.

