In article <4t5qfl$2cp@hermes.synopsys.com>, 
jbuck@synopsys.com (Joe Buck) writes:

|> Public-key cryptography seems the natural problem to put into a
|> benchmark; it does present export issues, but perhaps the benchmark can
|> simply consist of a series of kernels useful in public-key applications
|> with no actual crypto code.

I agree.  I see no reason why actual crypto code has to be 
in such a benchmark, and besides, crypto- people are by
no means the only people interested [from the Science
point of view, although from the commodity-PC point of
view, the other markets are probably small].

|> >So, I would urge you and others with similar requirements to agree
|> >on a representative benchmark of your requirements, write a
|> >portable, standard implementation of the benchmark, preferably
|> >in portable C, define what are allowable optimizations and 
|> >what are not, and benchmark some representative systems 
|> >yourselves.

|> "Portable C" may present a bit of a problem -- at minimum, some
|> host-dependent typedefs would be needed.  Given typedefs for two C
|> integral types, one of which is exactly twice the size of the other, a
|> portable arbitrary-precision arithmetic package can be written in C, but
|> the choice of what types to use is host-dependent (they should be the
|> largest types available on the processor).  For most hosts, a
|> substantially faster routine can be written in assembler (using
|> add-with-carry instructions that C compilers will generally not use).

I agree completely, 100%.  [Is that allowed on Usenet ;-)  ].
My suggestion about portable C was to provide a reference
or standard implementation, to provide a sanity check on
performance and results.  But, clearly, the whole point to such
a benchmark is to define a user interface such that really good
optimizations can be done underneath it, to encourage vendors 
to provide hardware support for fast performance.  Untouchable
portable C as the benchmark itself would defeat the entire purpose.
There should be a well-defined user interface, with any kind of
optimization, hardware or software, allowed underneath.

|> Perhaps some reasonable way can be found to proceed anyway.  One
|> possibility is to specify the interface of certain C-callable library
|> routines.  Vendors would be permitted to implement these routines however
|> they like, and portable programs could be written by using the standard
|> interfaces.  The GNU mp library interface is one possibility: there is
|> already a second implementation of this same interface, called FGMP,
|> because some people needed a non-GPLed implementation.  This library
|> contains all the arbitrary-precision arithmetic needed to do public-key
|> cryptography.

Interesting.  I was not aware of the mp/FGMP common interface.  
Is there a general consensus about the broad suitability of 
this?  [The fact that the library was re-implemented suggests yes...]
If so, this may be the right interface to build the benchmark on.



