>> 
>> One of the biggest problems with managing PCs is the fact that incompatible
>> addons - new graphics cards etc are constantly being produced. This means that
>> it is difficult to provide a centralised setup on a server which can be
>> downloaded and run properly on all PCs.
>> The NC will not provide this problem since such functions will probably be in
>> the firmware and applications will be downloaded in a device independent format
>> ie HTML and JAVA.  
>
>	The problem of course is if all the NC manufcturers can agree
>to one single standard in order to make network management simple.
>One way to achieve connectivity of course is to buy all the networked
>computers from a single source.  If so, why not just buy all the PCs
>from a company like HP and have then configure everything for you?
>
>- Chung

The point is there is no need for any  network management standard !!
Applications are passed to the NC in a platform independent format HTML, JAVA.
The specific hardware then displays this information on the devices it knows
best about - ie it's monitor in the best way for that device. This information
will in most cases be provided in the firmware on the box.
This is the whole point of the web philosophy !!

To a very large extent the NCs will be dumb terminals - what sort of management
will they need ?
As time progresses there will be hardware upgrades - better monitor etc.
This may mean that for an individual NC you may need when updating the hardware
to update the firmware. 
What other management is necessary ?

David Webb
VMS Systems Manager
Middlesex University




