: I am chasing almost any info on the MicroVAX3300 - some may become
: available for next to nothing RSN  and our computer club might want one or
: 2. Since I discovered this - I have to find out what freight will cost and
: whether they're worth the effort.

: Firstly: Physical size is the main concern for freight...
: 	 what box are they in, a BA23, BA123 ??
: 	 - more importantly, what do they weigh?

The 3300 is probably in the BA23 enclosure, but Q-Bus is Q-Bus and the
boards can be interchanged among BA23, BA123 and the newer enclosure
used in the VS3500.  I think the BA23 weighs in at around 50 lbs.

: Being a VAX-newbie, except for the VS2000 under my desk I intend to cut
: my teeth on, I am not really au fait with the hardware - 
: Would a 3300 have a SCSI controller in a stock machine, or were they 3rd
: party? And indeed would it just have an RQDXx MFM controller?

Almost surely there will not be a SCSI controller.  These are 3rd party
controllers and still quite expensive (around $1000).  You might luck
out and find an ESDI controller (but then you have to dig up the docs
for it).  ESDI controllers can usually handle full height ESDI drives
upwards of 500MB, and such drives are readily available at reasonable
prices.  The BA23 usually has just the RD54 drive and a TK50 drive.

: How does the KA640 compare to the uVAXII we have, CPU-performance-wise?
: (I realise neither of them are big number-crunchers)

It's about twice as fast as the KA630 MV II CPU.  But more importantly,
you can have more than 16MB of RAM with the KA640.

: - Real newbie Question, but worth asking, as we may get more than one:
: Are they clusterable and will they cluster under Ultrix-32?

Use Ultrix 4.2 or NetBSD.  I cluster these with massive NFS cross-mounts. 
You can also put DECnet and even PathWorks on them, too.  Basically the
MV III competes with the VS3100 in processing power.

