John Hascall (john@iastate.edu) wrote:
:     The VAX wasn't all that short on registers (unlike x86).

The VAX has 16 registers minus 2 or 3 which are used for special
purposes. You still have to think about the stack in writing assembly
because most VAX calling conventions passes parameters on the stack. Almost
every assembly language routine I've written on RISC machines (MIPS,
POWER/PowerPC, SPARC, Alpha, etc.) takes its arguments in registers, does
its computation in registers, and returns its value in a register. If the
routine is big enough that I have to buy a stack frame, the compiler
generally does better. (Unless one is writting asm becuase the compiler
doesn't do something you need to do.  Inline asm and builtin intrinsics
usually cover those cases pretty well though.)



