In article <4t0bpv$ido@dailyplanet.wam.umd.edu> veakblad@Glue.umd.edu (David T. Wang) writes:

|> I heard Larry Ellison's ranting and raving about his hatred of the PC on 
|> that PBS show, "Triumph of the Nerds".  Ofcourse he made the same point 
|> about how PC's are so terrible, etc etc, and everyone should use NC's.
|> One thing that struck me was that he neglected to mention that PC's though
|> more expensive, can be made to be just as "centrally configurable"
|> as NC's.  Ellison's great sales pitch was about how you would deliver 
|> software via a network to a NC, etc. etc. So, why couldn't you do this
|> to a PC?  The answer is, ofcourse you can do this to a PC. You've hit the
|> nail on the head when you just stated that the support costs far outweigh
|> the purchase cost. ergo, businesses would like easier to manage PC's, not
|> cheaper ones.  

First of all, the NC is more a paradigm than a machine. Just because Oracle's NC
demo machine is based on an ARM processor and runs Oracle's NCOS does not mean
that every NC should do the same. I believe Larry Ellison has said several times
- certainly he said it once when I was in the audience - that one possible NC
would be a Pentium-based PC minus disk drives. He has also said several times
that the OS on an NC should be irrelevant so the NC could well be running some
flavor of Windows. What is more important to the NC concept is that you should
be able to administer a bunch of machines from a central location, that software
should be downloadable on demand over a network rather than installed separately
on every single machine etc etc. 

|> ofcourse consumers would benefit with multiple suppliers, but do we really
|> need 5 major families of various philophies of RISC, 10+ minor families?
|> I would probably hope to see the industry converge to perhaps 3 major,
|> and 3 or 4 minor families, innovation could still come out of new
|> architectural advances, not just change the ISA for short term convieniences.

Well, we won't be having 5 major RISC families much longer. From what I have
heard, the Intel-HP P7 architecture (which is not a RISC architecture) will
replace both PA-RISC as well as x86. That leaves 4 and if one more drops out we
will be down to your desired number of three. IMHO, it is quite likely to
happen in the not too distant future. 

|> no quarrels here. Dec has always had decent, if not really good, technology.
|> but then many companies with clearly superior alternative technology
|> to the dominant, prevailing standards, have come and gone.  Dec certainly
|> brings a lot to the table, but can they execute? that remains to be seen.
|> Do they dump the entire x86 line and just go for broke, all Alpha? 
|> certainly interesting.

Don't see why they should dump their x86 line. Even though it isn't doing all
that well now, it could turn itself around. Having a profitable x86 line could
be a life-saver for DEC in case Alpha flops sometime in the future.

Raghu Mani

