I see you have a catch-all disclaimer.  Handy, that.


>I and my comrades at Intel will be quite happy to make
>future Intel CPUs beat Alpha on these codes [...]

Let's take an example: multiplying large integers.  The most time
critical part is multiplying words and accumulating the products.

Using Torbj�rn Granlund's code from GMP2:
On Alpha EV5, the inner loop takes 18 cycles per 64-bit word.
On Pentium Pro, it takes 9 cycles per 32-bit word.  Since the run-time
is O(n^2) for n words, that is equivalent to 36 cycles per 64 bits.

Alphas are now at 500Mhz.  But let's step back to the 417Mhz Alphas.
Heck, let's go back further, to the dim mists of time when Alphas were
clocked at 267MHz.

Now even if Intel's latest were clocked at 267MHz, it would be *half*
the speed of those old chips.


For large enough integers I use Karatsuba-Ofman's algorithm (as does
Torbj�rn).  That involves adding and subtracting large integers and
recursive calls that bottom out with the ordinary algorithm.  The
Alpha adds/subs at 4.75 cycles per 64 bits whereas the P6 takes 3.5
cycles per 32 bits.  Again the ancient Alpha soundly beats the P6.


Good thing you had that disclaimer, you can just reply that nobody is
interested in multiplying integers!  Would you prefer to talk about
floating point performance? (giggle)


I just want to run my stuff quickly so I would be *very* happy if
Intel (or anybody) came out with chips faster than Alphas.  However
your suggestion that "I and my comrades at Intel will be quite happy
to make future Intel CPUs beat Alpha on these codes" rings hollow
because the reality of the situation is that Intel isn't even in the
race.  If you could *triple* the P6 speed *today* you wouldn't even
catch up.

As seen as you are talking about vapourware, when is the next
generation of x86's due?  Two years away or so?  Alpha EV6 starts in
October.  Even faster clocks, 64-bit multiplication in 8 cycles,
pipelined so one can start *every* cycle, plus mucho other goodies...
I (and others) expect close to an order of magnitude improvement.
Meanwhile, P6 might catch up on that ancient 267MHz Alpha.


>If they are not interesting to much of the marketplace, 

Another disclaimer!


>or if the only
