"H.W. Stockman" <hwstock@swcp.com> writes:

>I suspect you are right, but out of curiosity, have you ever tried
>assembly language programming on a RISC system?  Now that assemblers
>are smarter, and the CPU's have sequencing hardware, the problems
>with load slots are not as severe.  But my one experience with asm
>on an R4000 was a bit sobering; the assembler reshuffled my instructions,
>often with bizarre results.  The OS writers clearly didn't want to give 
>control to asm users.  I don't think RISC systems are as friendly about
>writing asm (except perhaps under Linux...).

I have programmed the ARM in assembler, and found that quite easy. But
the ARM assembler doesn't rearrange instructions, as that, until the
StrongARM, has no effect on performance (except for avoiding overflow
in the write buffer). I agree that scheduling optimization should be
optional in an assembler. You might actually be able to do a better
job than the assembler. ;-)

In addition to the ARM, I've programmed assembler on 6502, Z80 and
PDP-11. I found the ARM as easy or easier to program as the PDP-11,
and (of course) far easier than any of the 8-bits.

	Torben Mogensen (torbenm@diku.dk)




