Robert Harley wrote:

> >What are the problems?
> 
> Arithmetic with big numbers and polynomials, mostly.

Robert was talking about 64-bit integers mainly, and certainly has
a point for that application.  I almost went to 64-bit MIPs/alpha
for another reason -- the ability to operate on 8 8-bit lattice-gas
cells packed into a 64-bit word at once -- but that's an area where
MMX will probably be faster than general 64-bit RISC regsiters.

But here's another point about big numbers.  Back in 1979, the IEEE
was urging people to go to 80-bit precision for floating point,
and Intel has carried the 80-bit torch for a long time.  When I'm
not insisting on IEEE compatibility, I compile my programs to allow
80-bit internal precision, and it seems to let some routines
converge more quickly.  It might be interesting to inject some 
routines into SPEC that benefit from higher internal precision, and
allow such architechtures to use it in benchmarks.

After all, there WAS a reason the IEEE pushed for 80-bit, and it does
put an extra penalty on those who design chips for compatibility with the
80-bit standard.
-- 
