    This is not criticism per se, but, I would like to observe that
    over the last decade a lot of high-precision arithmetic folks 
    have had numerous and various architectural complaints, but, 
    so far, there has never been a serious benchmarking effort
    that I, personally, am aware of.  By "serious", I mean something 
    like: Linpack, SPEC, STREAM, GPC, TPC, NAS parallel benchmarks, 
    etc., etc.  

I'll go further: I think that the time may have come to make high
precision arithmetic run fast - because cryptography is about to
become ubiquitous.

Max Domeika, a student working for me, has been working on turning PGP
encryption into a form suitable for inclusion in SPEC98. Barring legal
and export problems, we're further ahead than any other SPEC98
candidate. So, as such, there will be an application that includes a
significant amount of multiple precision arithmetic in a near-future
benchmark suite.

But, the extended precision algorithms and libraries in use are, umm,
somewhat naive.

I welcome the assistance of anyone from the high precision arithmetic
community in developing the PGP encryption benchmark, any other form
of encryption benchmark, or any other significant application of
multiprecision arithemtic as a benchmark.

Oh, and, yes: I expect that Alpha will have an advantage in
multiprecision arithmetic because of 64 bits.  That's why I am working
on it. First develop a realistic benchmark, then make Intel run faster
on it than anyone else. :-)

No, I will engage in no benchmarketing. I just find it rather specious
to tune a CPU for imaginary and reputed and rumoured application
benefits.




