	This is correct. You can not use HARDWARE mirroring across
	independend controllers. They do not know about one another.
	You CAN use software mirroring through LSM on Digital Unix
	or SW-RAID software under VMS, and through the disk administator
	under NT,

> This is most puzzling since previously we've disk mirrors on 
>other systems such as HP & Sun on separate controllers without problem.  

	I'd be rather surprised. Perhaps they were not talking	
	about the same thing, or let's say hidding the details better.

>The controller in question is the 3-port PCI Backplane RAID Controller 
>model KZPSC-BA.  The DEC Systems & Options Catalog also mentions that the 
>internal storage assemblies can be configured for split bus mode to 
>support wide mode on the first bus and narrow mode on the second bus.  

The picture you included was not entirely clear to me as it suggested
all three controllers scsi busses going to a single bay with 8 disks.
The 2100's I've seen only allow for a 2 way split of the bays into
two groups of 4, allowing only for two scsi cables to be connected.
So you'd would not be able to used the 3rd port on either controller
internally. Actually, you wouldn't used the 1rst ports, because that
is accesible externally allowing you to grow externally in future.
Narrow/Wide essentially does not enter into this picture. That's all
a matter of negotiating at the SCSI level, once basic compatibility 
criteria are satisfied. Ofcourse wide has the potential to go faster.
Using Software mirroting look like Digital UNIXs LSM you can indicate
the controller with the wide drives to be the 'preferred' one directing
the IOs there (unless a deep queue builds).

