lamaster@viking.arc.nasa.gov (Hugh LaMaster) writes:

> This is not criticism per se, but, I would like to observe that over the
> last decade a lot of high-precision arithmetic folks have had numerous and
> various architectural complaints, but, so far, there has never been a
> serious benchmarking effort that I, personally, am aware of.  By "serious",
> I mean something like: Linpack, SPEC, STREAM, GPC, TPC, NAS parallel
> benchmarks, etc., etc.

> Where an individual, academic group, or industry consortium takes
> responsibility for developing a clean, well-defined, mostly portable - and
> extremely well-defined where not portable - benchmark, and takes the time to
> collect carefully, publish, and publicize, the results over an extended
> period of time.  My experience has been that many companies will respond,
> and eventually, the benchmark will even have an impact on new
> implementations, if a serious effort is undertaken.  But, random complaints
> posted to Usenet will have little effect, because it is not clear to an
> outside observer whether there is an objective measure to see how well an
> implementation is doing relative to the competition.  Without that, there is
> nothing in it for the vendors: without an independent, third-party appraisal
> of how well they are doing, there is no reward for performing well.
>
> So, I would urge you and others with similar requirements to agree on a
> representative benchmark of your requirements, write a portable, standard
> implementation of the benchmark, preferably in portable C, define what are
> allowable optimizations and what are not, and benchmark some representative
> systems yourselves.  Collect and publish results that others send to you.
> Eventually, you will probably have an impact, but it will take a few years
> to have an impact.


I AGREE!  From inside Motorola & HP looking out, we target compiler
optimizations to get good benchmark numbers first - because there is
a clear-cut reward, and general optimizations second.  Spec95 is
complex enough that optimizations that help it tend to help other
codes as well.  But special idioms like multi-precision math don't
show up as hotspots in any benchmark, and good multi-precision math
performance doesn't appear to sell many systems today.  A widely
visible benchmark might change that.


