And it seems to me that byte manipulation is a lot slower on Alpha,
which also proves nothing by itself. Of course more people run word
processing than polynomial solutions, what you need is a good
polynomial game.

| >If they are not interesting to much of the marketplace, 
| 
| Another disclaimer!

But it's a point well taken, Intel is selling to the mass market,
not the very top end, not to niche applications. No offense, but
what you do is NOT a big market.

The P7 will address the 64 bit market, but 64 bits is very close to
the point of diminishing returns. There are a lot of problems you
can't solve with 8 bit CPUs, and many you can't solve with 16 bits.
But today virtually all major apps are on 32 bit platforms, and use
some software to handle 64 bit ints. So the native 64 bit machines
will perform better for 64 bit apps, and make 128 bit apps
practical, but each time you go bigger the number of new apps you
can solve gets smaller, and the cost of support chips and memory
gets larger.

I'm guessing that 64 bit systems will not push 32 bit out of the
market the way 16 bit systems went away, and that 64 bits will be
the limit of what sells in the mass market, simply because of the
limited number of cases where more than 32 bits is in any way
useful. Unless vendors decide to force 32 bits off the market by
price pressure, I would expect half the mass market to still be 32
bit five years from now. The average users doesn't have the
application which needs it, and will need to be pressured by
marketing to spend more for 64 bit.
