    Why don't you ask Analog Devices?  They have technology sharing
    agreements on some of IBM SiGe technologies.  In fact they have a
    standard product, a GHz range D/A converter IRRC, that uses it.
    I would be suprised if it wasn't being designed into some of
    your company's next generation ATE and T&M equipment right now.
 
>Does Josephson junction or GaAs mean anything to you? I seem to recall

    As far as I know no one is currently pursuing JJ logic for
    computer applications.

    GaAs as been long touted as the "technology of the future" and
    it will be for a long time.  It is cost effective in RF stages
    for wireless but its use in computers is rather restricted.
    Cray Computer Co. bet its life on the viability of entirely
    GaAs CPUs and lost.  The critical point in high performance
    computing is how much logic and cache you can put on a single
    die.  Once your critical path crosses chip boundaries then
    your performance is killed regardless of whether your on chip
    logic gates switch in 100 ps or 200 ps.  Unfortunately for
    GaAs, the level of integration available has significantly
    lagged silicon, especially CMOS.

