> The PC Imperative
> 
> The fact that Bruce Claflin remains at the helm of the PCBU�albeit with a
> strong mandate to produce positive results Real Soon Now�indicates that
> DEC management still has confidence in the VP�s abilities. Similarly, both
> Palmer and Mullarkey emphasized the continued strategic importance of
> Intel-inside platforms. Stating that the firm�s WNT game plan renders PCs
> a �strategic necessity,� Palmer noted the importance of providing a
> complete PC solution that includes desktop and mobile systems. �Our
> strategic focus is on servers but we will continue to provide client
> systems to enterprise customers. We have to find a way to do it that is
> less painful,� he said.
> 
> When it comes to PC migraine attacks, outsourcing may be the most powerful
> pain reliever that DEC can buy. Doctor Palmer apparently is ready to write
> just such a prescription, as indicated by his observation that Digital �no
> longer has the scale left to justify manufacturing� its own desktops.
> Subcontracting client systems would allow the company to focus its efforts
> on Prioris servers, which offer greater profit margin and differentiation
> opportunities than do commodity desktops. Unstated by Palmer was the fact
> that the retrenchment validates his longstanding view of the PC
> marketplace: during a discussion with SKD nearly two years ago, DEC�s CEO
> expressed strong reservations about entering the consumer PC business.
> 
> Euro-trashed Sales and Strategy
> 
> Europe, which now represents at least 43 percent of Digital�s business, is
> another trouble spot. Due in part to an economic downturn in Germany and
> France, DEC�s 4FQ96 European revenues will be at least $150M below plan. A
> $50M 4FQ96 revenue decline didn�t help, either. More troubling is the fact
> that DEC�s transition to an indirect channels strategy in Europe left
> money on the table and resulted in the loss of customers. Briefly, the
> problem centers on the fact that DEC has been unable to effectively
> recruit and leverage channels partners on the European continent.
> Althought DEC appears to be doing well with its European channels (see
> table on Page 3), a dearth of pan-European distributors, compounded by the
> fact that European customers prefer direct vendor contact, has forced DEC
> to reconstitute its direct-sales force.
> 
> Flummoxed by Fluctuations
> 
> DEC has been hammered�in a very big way�by a stronger-than-expected dollar
> in Europe and the Far East alike. The aggregate negative impact on FY96
> earnings is likely to exceed $150M.
>  
> To Get Leaner, You Gotta Be Meaner
> While SKD has long maintained that Digital�s headcount ultimately would
> stabilize at or below the 50K level, Bob Palmer and other senior
> executives have indicated on many occasions that 60K was �about the right
> size� for DEC. These statements, however, invariably were qualified with a
> �barring changes in business or market conditions� caveat. Changes quite
> obviously have occurred.
> 
> Although DEC�s current headcount of ~59K represents a 55 percent reduction
> from a peak employee population of ~130K, the firm�s revenue per employee
> is about $240K, well below the ~$310K industry average. Moreover, DEC�s 6
> percent operating profit is only about half that posted by IBM and HP. The
> impending 7K headcount reduction will boost revenue per employee to about
> $270K, still well below the competition. Over half the proposed layoffs
> will affect overhead and infrastructure positions, thus portending more
> business unit integration and perhaps a downsized business unit org chart.
> (DEC�s decision to abdicate the $125M memory business, coupled with
> slumping printer sales, portends the dissolution of the CPBU).
> 
> SKD anticipates that the majority of the reductions will occur within the
> ABU and SBU where a recent consolidation has resulted in a large number of
> overlapping  jobs. One possible action: DEC will open every position
> within the restructured SBU to competitive bidding. Current employees will
> be invited to bid on the newly-created jobs. Palmer�s statement that DEC
> will refocus its service strategy on medium and large scale companies
> indicates that the newly-formed Services Division won�t be completely
> immune to downsizing, either.
> 
> The Not-So-Bad News
> 
> Despite the embarassment of a significant rewrite in the sustained
> profitability story, a less-than-laudatory reassessment by Wall Street, 
> and the morale problems associated with yet another workforce decimation,
> DEC has a few things going for it. On July 1, Digital Semiconductor
> announced a substantial boost in high-end Alpha horsepower� Alpha
> performance is up 70 percent since the November �95 introduction of the
> Pentium Pro�as well as hefty price reductions on existing EV5 and EV56
> chips. If the savings are passed along to the SBU, the repricing
> initiative should enable DEC to compete more effectively in the
> workstation and midrange server markets where it faces increasing
> competition from Sun, HP, Compaq, et al.
> 
> As in 3FQ96, the bulk of DEC�s strategic businesses experienced
> double-digit growth in the quarter justed ended. Mullarkey estimated that
> Alpha sales grew at least 30 percent year-over-year, and that WNT and Unix
> exhibited even stronger growth rates. Storage is another bright spot:
> StorageWorks is now a $1.4B business that boasts an enviable
> revenue-per-employee of $1M. Finally, DEC in 4FQ96 landed three
> workstation contracts worth as much as $500M. Late last month, DEC
> garnered a three-year contract with Citibank that will swell the firm�s
> coffers by a similar amount.
> 
> The Road Ahead
> 
> During the May 7 AltaVista product launch, Palmer observed that Digital�s
> recovery remained a work in progress, and that much heavy lifting remained
> to be done. �In a restructuring of this magnitude... sometimes you take
> two steps forward and three steps back.� During a July 3 interview with
> SKD, Palmer expressed confidence in the firm�s ability to make continued
> progress, and admitted that both he and Digital �are still learning� as
> they progress along the transformation continuum. Channels 101 is a core
> subject in the CEO�s curriculum, and we have ample reason to expect that
> Mr. Palmer will devote close personal attention to better managing
> Digital�s relationships with its key channel partners.
> 
> Palmer Out to Pasture?
> 
> According to the stock market and the trade press, things look pretty ugly
> at DEC right now. Palmer expressed hope that Digital will measured on its
> progress thus far as well as the current downturn, but the slip on the
> comeback trail certainly exposes the CEO. While he deserves credit for
> confronting the situation in a timely and straightforward manner, rather
> than deferring the bad news until 1FQ97, it�s obvious that Palmer once
> again finds himself on academic probation. But the CEO has been in this
> unenviable position before, and there�s no compelling reason to believe
> that he will be unable to restore himself and his firm to good standing
> yet again.
> 
> -- 
