cyang@scdt.intel.com (Chung-Hsuing Yang) wrote:
[snip]
>	But the size of CPU core unit is really a matter of
>design philosophy.  DECs approach is to make the simplest
>architecture  possible in order to push the clock rate as high 
>as possible.  Even though at the architecture level, the 
>thoughput isn't as good, they hope that high clock rate would
>translate into better performance.  If you look at their assembly
>code, you'll basically see lots ans lots of NOPS. They are able to 

   I think you may have been looking at either MIPS code compatible
   with early CPUs, system level Alpha "PAL" code (e.g. access to
   the implementation dependent system registers in the 21064 was
   not interlocked and you will see a lot of NOPs here), or code
   specific to 21164 4 way issue requirements.

   The Alpha itself is interlocked at the user code level (e.g.
   load delays) and doesn't support delayed branching so I don't
   think you will see nearly as many NOPs as with some other RISCs.
   The Alpha will tend to interlock a lot during execution but that
   is the price paid for not supporting speculative execution.
 
>achieve high clock rate in earlier Alphas by limiting their maximum 
>logic depth to six.  As you can imaging, they are not able to do 
>complicated decoding with that.  On the other hand, Intel and HP
>sought to take a more balanced approach, better architecture at 

   I have to disagree here.  What is unbalanced about the Alpha?
   Its ratio of integer to FP performance places it between the
   relatively FP poor P6 and the relatively int poor PA-8000.  It
   operates internally at great speed but it is fairly mundane at
   the system interface level.  The Alpha strives for an efficient
   on-chip cache hierarchy to reduce impact on the rest of the
   system.

   Compared to the Alpha, the P6 and PA-8000 are more difficult to
   build systems around.  The P6 uses a non-standard signalling
   system (GTL+) and a very deeply pipelined bus.  The PA-8000
   requires large, wide external caches operating at the CPU clock
   frequency.

   How do you judge x86 and PA to be "better" architectures than
   Alpha?  Given the respective implementations (21164, P6, PA-8000)
   the Alpha is a less expensive at the chip level, simpler at the
   system level, dominates in integer performance, and matches the
   PA-8000 in FP.  It could be argued that the Alpha ISA is better
   than x86 & PA because it permits this superior implementation.

>the expense of lower clock rate ( that is not strictly true, 
>you always try to run things as far as possible.  But more complex
>logic makes pushing clock rate a little more difficult. However
>better fab technology can ease that trade-off. )
>
>	In terms of architecture HP-PA guys are very respected among
>academic circles.  Their machine are very popular and very fast! 

     And HP is even more respected by businesses.  They have
     consistently made high quality, competitive RISC machines
     since their orginal "snakes" workstation caught everyone's
     attention.

     They have taken a virtually polar opposite approach from Alpha
     by offloading almost all caches from their high end CPUs.  HP
     tries to make up for the resulting clock frequency limitations
     by increasing the work done per clock with duplicated functional
     units and wide instruction issue.  This approach has paid off
     in some ways but at the cost of flexibility in targeting a wide
     range of price/performance points and very costly system level
     implementations.

>(oh, we at Intel are pretty good too!).   DEC leverage off

     The P6 has demonstrated a suprisingly high performance for
     an x86 implementation.  The P6 reminds me of the aeronautical
