If Andy G. is losing sleep, it isn't for Alpha, MIPS, SPARC, or even
PowerPC. Outside of the embedded market (where Alpha and P6 are totally
irrelevant anyway) Intel would gain relatively little by taking over all
competing microprocessor sales. (Being incredibly generous, Alpha may sell
200,000 chips per year. That is not overwhelmingly interesting to Intel.)

Intel's main concerns are to keep the overall growth of the PC market high,
to grab more of the dollars inside a PC for Intel CPUs, and to create
entirely new markets. Competing with AMD and Cyrix, etc. is likely much
more of a priority than worrying about DEC. Somewhere down below "What am I
going to have for lunch today?" Andy G. lets Alpha cross his mind I
suppose.



