>result, their process technology is leading edge and generally

    Their process technology has to be leading edge to stay within
    50% or higher of leading edge RISC integer performance while
    dragging their heavy x86 baggage along with them.  As apps become
    more portable Intel is going to have close the gap to avoid
    significant customer defections.  The P7 may just arrive in the
    nick of time (if every thing works out) to avoid widespread
    defection.

    A lot of Intel's R&D budget goes into design and verification
    of CPUs rather than process development.  The x86 requires at
    least twice the number of logic transistors as leading RISC
    designs to stay within 50% of integer performance (and far, far
    behind on FP) even with the expensive process and system level
    tricks Intel does but the RISC vendors can avoid.  The complex
    and byzantine nature of the x86 ISA forces Intel to use far
    more engineering design effort, especially with the superscalar
    and speculative techniques needed to keep pace with RISC
    performance.

    Finally, most of Intel's capital investment is simply keeping
    its huge capacity fitted with leading edge processes.  The RISC
    makers probably couldn't fill more than 10% of the PC market if
    Intel suddenly dropped dead tomorrow.  But if RISC did capture
    10% it would represent a huge new source of revenue in addition
    to workstations and servers, especially as it would be in the
    high end, where most of the profit in PC hardware lies.

