"significant customer defections"? What the heck are you talking about?

If you didn't sound so earnest, I'd think this was one giant troll.
You sound like the unarmed guy who is surrounded by an army pointing their
guns and who asks "Give up yet?"

You haven't yet presented a single reason for any customer to
switch from Intel to DEC. Certainly not integer performance. That
whole game is one of leapfrog and while they may or may not be 
leading at any given time, Intel is clearly in the game. Besides,
the conventional wisdom is that a factor of 4 in price/performance
is needed before anyone will consider switching architectures and
DEC doesn't have that.
Floating point performance is one area but there is no indication
I can see that lots of NT users are doing any serious FP computations
anyway.
It can't be price. Intel drops their prices like clockwork.
They won't be beaten there. So what's left?

And besides the points above, you've not addressed the issue that
*despite* the fact NT is available, there is very little software
available on NT for Alpha, relatively speaking.

>    A lot of Intel's R&D budget goes into design and verification
>    of CPUs rather than process development.  The x86 requires at
>    least twice the number of logic transistors as leading RISC
>    designs to stay within 50% of integer performance (and far, far
>    behind on FP) even with the expensive process and system level

You are missing the point I made. If Intel gets to the next process
generation ahead of everyone else, they have a tremendous advantage, 
regardless of the ISA. Lower power consumption, faster clocks, 
bigger caches, better yields. Do you think this fact is lost on them?
Do you wonder why their process technology spending dwarfs everyone
else's? 

Think about it like this:
Say a RISC architecture gives you a 50% speed advantage over the
x86 architecture using the same clock and number of transistors.

Now let's start moving through time, iterating over smaller and
smaller devices. Your 50% RISC advantage does not change with time.
But if I get a 25% advantage in performance by moving to each new
process generation, and you are not moving as fast, it is only
a matter of time until I am faster than you, use less power than
you, have better yields than you, and cost less than you.

Pat



