H.W. Stockman (hwstock@swcp.com) wrote:
: I suspect you are right, but out of curiosity, have you ever tried
: assembly language programming on a RISC system?

Yes, a fair bit actually. Its a lot easier than either the VAX or x86
because you can generally keep everything in registers.

:  Now that assemblers
: are smarter, and the CPU's have sequencing hardware, the problems
: with load slots are not as severe.  But my one experience with asm
: on an R4000 was a bit sobering; the assembler reshuffled my instructions,
: often with bizarre results.  The OS writers clearly didn't want to give 
: control to asm users. 

No. The compiler group at MIPS decided to build instruction reordering into
the assembler so that asm programmers could take advantage of it. For cases
where the precise instruction order is important, such as in low-level OS
code where there are timing and side effect considerations the assembler
doesn't know about, one uses an assembler directive to turn off reordering.

: I don't think RISC systems are as friendly about
: writing asm (except perhaps under Linux...).

Well if it is easier under one OS than another, then perhaps your idea of
"easiness" is a property of software, not the computer architecture. I've
seen braindead assemblers for many architectures...



