   for DOS-Windows compatibility and start looking at the competition
   it is quickly apparent that x86 CPUs suck big-time.  If you need
   a generic "125 MIPs" of integer performance what are you going to
   pick for your incredibly cost sensitive, possibly battery powered
   product? a ~$35 1/2 W StrongARM, a ~$35 1.8 W 64 bit MIPS R4300i,
   or a ~$150 10+ W (worst case) Pentium P54/100 (which also requires
   a wider and faster bus)? ROTFLMAO!
   

>more of a priority than worrying about DEC. Somewhere down below "What am I
>going to have for lunch today?" Andy G. lets Alpha cross his mind I
>suppose.

   What does concern Andy G. these days?  Well lets see, business is
   starting a big transition from the x86-only Win 3.1/95 to the pro-
   cessor independent Win NT OS on the high end.  Here, Intel will be
   experiencing its most important architectural transition (to the P7)
   since the 8086 was introduced, at the time DEC will be targeting
   low cost and faster Alphas into the same NT market, backed by the
   manufacturing muscle of Mitsubishi and Samsung.  On the low end is
   the growing threat of a plethora of new consumer electronics based
   on cheap and powerful processors like MIPS, ARM, and PPC.  All
   the while, the x86 cloners will be forcing Pentium margins down
   and increasingly desperate PC makers start looking for alternatives
   to the Intel hegemony.

   Sounds like a regular day at the office.

Paul

All opinions strictly my own.

