haertel@ichips.intel.com (Mike Haertel) wrote:
>In article <vwj4tn9je3p.fsf@osfb.aber.ac.uk>,
>Piercarlo Grandi <pcg@aber.ac.uk> wrote:
>>Obviously untrue: the Pentium Pro is a latter day VAX/9000, and by
>>spending huge silicon budgets, it competes rather well with most
>
>Of course I'm a biased opinion, but I disagree on two counts:
>
>1.  The P6 has a lot more in common with the VAX/8700 than it does
>    with either the VAX/8600, VAX/9000, or Pentium.  Specifically,
>    the 8600 and 9000 supposedly were heavily pipelined implementations
>    of the VAX *macro*architecture, and the Pentium is a pipelined
>    implementation of the 386 *macro*architecture.  By contrast the
>    8700 and the P6 pipelines are optimized to execute an internal
>    microinstruction set, and macroinstructions are first explicitly
>    translated to the internal representation.
>
>2.  The Pentium Pro is not a whole lot larger than most of its
>    RISC competitors in similar processes, and smaller than some.
>--
>Mike Haertel <haertel@ichips.intel.com>
>Not speaking for Intel.

    How do you mean size? Die area or transistor count?

    If you look at processor logic transistors (i.e. ignore on-chip
    cache) the P6 weighs in at about 4.3 million while the Alpha
    21164 is a rather svelte 1.8 million or so.  Even the MIPS R10K,
    which is just about aggressive as the P6 in out of order and
    speculative execution, uses only about 2.2 million transistors.

    So your x86 *baggage* seems to be around a factor of 2x in
    complexity.

    BTW, HP seems to learning bad habits from its collaboration
    with you guys, the PA-8000 uses about 3.9 million logic
    transistors.   ;-)

    I guess you would rather talk about die size!  Seeing as you
    have the most aggressive metalization rules, BJTs for big
    drivers, moderate clock rates, and volumes at least 10x
    bigger than any RISC chip so you can afford to burn man-years
    playing diddle the polygon.  Funny though - the 21164 reaches
    500 MHz in pure CMOS (i.e. BIG drive stages), packs 7 times
    the cache on die as the P7, is built with looser interconnect
    pitch, and still comes in at a respectable 209 mm2 vs 197 mm2
    for the P6 (and a bloated 345 mm2 for the PA-8000).

Paul

All opinions strictly my own.

