zalman@netcom.com (Zalman Stern) wrote:
>Paul DeMone (pdemone@tundra.com) wrote:
>:     Don't forget that the new P7 64 bit ISA will require an new suite
>:     of system software like compilers, debuggers and OS's.
>
>I'm willing to bet that the next generation x86 family part from Intel will
>boot Windows 95 and Windows NT 3.51 and run all the apps on my disk just
>fine. This is a no-brainer as far as I'm concerned. How Intel accomplishes
>this on the other hand will likely be quite interesting.

    Yes the P7 will be backwardly compatible with the existing x86
    code base.  But what I said was for the "P7 64 bit ISA" i.e. the
    native code of the new Intel/HP architecture, will require a new
    software base.  The reason Intel is dumping x86 is because they
    have already played most of the possible implementation tricks to
    get higher performance and they now have to leap ahead 20 years
    in computer architecture design to keep up with Alpha, PA-RISC,
    SPARC, and MIPS roadmaps.  x86 applications will have to be
    recompiled into P7 64 bit native code for maximum benefit.
>
>That we are even having an argument about 366 MHz 21164 performance vs.
>Pentium Pro 200 MHz performance means Intel has flat out won this battle as

