>>>>>> "Pascal" == Pascal Dornier <pdornier@shellx.best.com> writes:
>In article <31E7F808.7F5D@shellx.best.com> Pascal Dornier <pdornier@shellx.best.com> writes:
>    Pascal> The digitally tweaked power supply causes _almost_ no cost
>    Pascal> increase, and greatly enhances flexibility. One of the few
>    Pascal> things about P6 that I like.
>
>would someone be kind enough to expand on this ?  it sounds interesting
>and i know nothing about it....   /j
>-- 
>Jeff Tuckey		ext. 3739

    The P6 has four voltage identification pins called VID[3:0].  These
    encode 15 different voltage levels according to the following table:

         VID[3:0]     Voltage Setting (VccP pins)

           0000              3.5 V
           0001              3.4 V
           0010              3.3 V
           ...                ...
           1101              2.2 V
           1110              2.1 V
           1111              CPU not present

    The 16th value is used to detect the absence of a CPU in the
    socket (i.e. all four signals pulled high by on board resistors).
    The motherboard is expected to have a power supply regulator for
    each CPU socket to provide the indicated voltage with +/- 5%
    tolerance.

    It is an interesting method to insure that future processors
    requiring a lower power supply voltage (i.e process shrink) can
    be used in a existing motherboard.

    The P6 motherboard also requires a separate low impedance 1.5V
    power supply to provide termination for P6's "GTL+" signals.


Paul

