In article <4shiht$ed5@news.iastate.edu>,
John Hascall <john@iastate.edu> wrote:
>Zalman Stern <zalman@netcom.com> wrote:
>}H.W. Stockman (hwstock@swcp.com) wrote:
>}: why has is been
>}: so damn hard to get "optimized" Word and Excel... or is it that
>}: those apps, which do lots of character-oriented instructions,
>}: are really not that great guns on RISC?

Most of the slowness of porting applications from x86 to RISC is not
anything to do with RISC. It has to do with applications being written
in assembly code, or being written in C relying on non-standard
features of a particular compiler. If an application is only ever
intended to run on Wintel, no effort is put into making it portable,
and the first port is very hard.

The ISV's who care about performance on Alpha are companies like
Oracle and Sybase and Informix, and you'll see that Alpha does quite
well on their code, because they put in the effort to tune it. The
performance of Word and Excel on Alpha hasn't been a priority for
Microsoft, because that isn't where their market is. Applications
where performance is an important selling point (like CAD) do tend to
be tuned for Alpha when they are ported, and they run well.

In short, if the company that owns the source code cares about
performance, it tends to make sense for them to spend money on porting
to Alpha NT because that is where they can get the best
performance. If a company owns the source code to a DOS application
where features are more important than performance, it makes more
sense to spend their R&D money on feature-building than on porting and
tuning for a low-volume processor, no matter how fast it is.

>}
>}Please replace that with "...on Alpha." Most RISC architectures do just
>}fine manipulating 8-bit bytes.
>
>    Or more precisely, "...on early iterations of Alphas."
>
>}                               (And in actuality, Alpha can do well there
>}too if the code is written to do 64-bit loads and stores. This is really
>}hard to do in a lot of cases though.)

The DEC compilers for Alpha know how to issue very good code for doing
byte operations, even on early implementations. In some cases, we use
64-bit loads and stores to do "vector" operations on bytes. Even when
that is not possible, considerable effort is spent to merge several
byte operations on the same word into one memory load/store. That this
technology works when it can be applied is borne out by the fact that
Alpha has led the Specint race pretty much since it came to
market.

In general, Alpha is a "fast-tick" architecture: the architects
believe that making byte-accesses relatively expensive is still a win
if it means the clock can be sped up enough to make everything else go
really, really fast. Complementary compiler technology is used to
optimize byte ops on a word-based memory system, to lessen the effect
of making some operations more costly.
