John Pieper (pieper@mink.zko.dec.com) wrote:
: In general, Alpha is a "fast-tick" architecture: the architects
: believe that making byte-accesses relatively expensive is still a win
: if it means the clock can be sped up enough to make everything else go
: really, really fast. Complementary compiler technology is used to
: optimize byte ops on a word-based memory system, to lessen the effect
: of making some operations more costly.

Circa 1993-1994, the app I was working on was ported to Alpha NT. A 150 MHz
21064 delivered about 10% better than a Pentium 90 MHz on byte oriented
image processing code. I ran a fair bit of the inner loop code through
compilers for Alpha on NT and OSF and for anything interesting, I was
seeing full load/compute/merge/store sequences in the inner loop. (The
situation was made much worse by the less than stellar integer multiply
performance on the 21064 and that a 64k lookup table didn't help because
that depended on byte and word ops.)

I bounced this off a couple people at DEC and got responses that the only
solution was to rewrite the code to do explicit word operations. I
explained what this involved to our marketing manager and he rightly said
to forget it as if we sold a copy of the app for every Alpha NT box in
existence it still wouldn't have been interesting revenue.

Its now a couple years down the road. Alpha has perhaps become accpetable
for this kind of code. Meanwhile, Intel has speced MMX which delivers a 2x
to 4x speedup in many pixel twiddling situations.




