In article <4s4ld3$3jh@nntp1.best.com>, infowerk@nntp.best.com (infowerk) writes:

>I have a hard time to restore from a tape to a 3rd party hard-Drive (like
>seagate & fujitsu and etc.) and it's because of the disk is not recognized
>(things like rpm ans stuff) in the /etc/disktab file, where can I get
>these disktab updates?

Digital doesn't include 3rd party disks in /etc/disktab.  But all
versions of Digital UNIX and V4.0 and later of ULTRIX will query
the disk for its geometry and use the default partition table from
the driver.  This does require a disk that is well behaved.  While
most SCSI-2 disks are there are some which aren't and these may
be causing problems.  On Digital you can label the disk first and
that will get the geometry.

# disklabel -wr /dev/rrz#a whatever

On ULTRIX, the newfs command will get the geometry itself.

# newfs /dev/rrz#a /dev/rrz#a

In the case of Digital UNIX and the rotational speed, it always
fills in 3600 rpm because the interface used to get the geometry
doesn't have a field for getting the speed.  This shouldn't be
a serious problem since the UFS rotational delay is the only
consumer I know of for the rotational speed and you don't need
to use it most modern disks.

>

