>Seems the RISC camp is always saying "Wait till next year".  You have

    Next year? No the 500 MHz Alpha 21164 is sampling now with production
    in September and it will easily top Klamath.  BTW, I just heard the
    Klamath has slipped another quarter to 2Q97.  The 21264 will likely
    be announced this fall (likely sampling at announcement, with product-
    ion early next year given DEC's exemplary track record in this regards).

>the Specmarks for the P7, correct?  You have the actual ship date,
>correct?

    No, the P7 is mostly vapour at this time.  And giving that it
    involves an architectural transition, it has much more chance
    in slipping out than the P5 or P6.  Intel says 1998 but I think
    they will work hard to suprise everyone and bring it in late next
    year, unless development problems or performance shortfalls turn
    up (i.e. it "pulls a 620" ;-)

    Don't forget that the new P7 64 bit ISA will require an new suite
    of system software like compilers, debuggers and OS's.  How long
    will it take for ISVs to port to native P7?  Look how long the x86
    16 to 32 bit code transition took (or I should say, is taking!).
    With at least a 5 year head start over the P7, the Alpha is becoming
    the well-entrenched leader in 64 bit computing.

