Theo Vosse wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> 
> I wrote in a program:
> 
> #include <ctype.h>
> 
>     if (isalpha(*str))
> 
> And the compiler gave this awful warning:
[... stuff deleted ...]
> Is this normal, or does it have anything to do with the -xtaso_short
> option? I do not have any documentation with exception of the man pages, since
> DEC seems to be economical with documentation when you're under a campus license...

It has _everything_ to do with the -xtaso_short option.   The system
interfaces
are always 64 bits, and the system header files are not protected from
the
effects of the -xtaso_short option.   The Programmers Guide (which you
obviously
don't have) explains all this.  (It also gives a script you can use to
change
the headers on your system) If you change your program to:

#pragma pointer_size (save)
#pragma pointer_size (long)
#include <ctype.h>
#pragma pointer_size (restore)

[...]

It should work as you expect.

