>(regarding Bapco's recent tests of NT on a 200mhz Pro vs. 366mhz
>Alpha)
>
>ROTFL!  Do you know where 64-bit comes into play, Ian?  For huge
>several-gigabyte style databases, yes-for the software they ran- no.
>The only aspect of the test where the Alpha beat the Pro was in the
>CAD test where it's floating point abilities could be used- and that
>was only about 20% improvement.

    The use of 64 bit integers is also very useful in CAD software
    and can speed up event driven and cycle-based simulation.  64 bit
    integers can also speed up bit field operations for things like
    data flow analysis in compilers and cryptographic applications.

    The use of 64 bit logical addresses gives the power to implement
    single level models of storage and permit sparse allocation of
    pointers to efficiently implement capabilities in object-based
    systems.  Large MCAD and ECAD databases require >4 GB addressing
    to permit tools to model and simulate complete large systems
    instead of requiring error-prone piece-wise analysis.

    Re: 64 vs 32 bit CPUS; "The single worst mistake that can be made 
    in computer architecture design is too few addressing bits; unlike
    most mistakes, it can seldom be recovered from".  BTW, addressing
    needs increase by about one address bit per 1.5 years.
>
>
>>And the P6 *should* have beaten the Alpha (the P6 is a much newer chip). The
>>fact that it didn't merely demonstrates the robustness of Digital's design
>>methodology.
>
>A "newer" chip?  What ridiculous logic is this?  The Alpha was a
>366mhz model. It cost signicantly more than the Pro.  It was the
>fastest chip available at the time the test was done.
>
>>These arguments tire me no end. You lose the argument on performance and so you
>>move on to cost.
>
>Cost is _everything_.  Whoops, looks like Alpha has lost!  Cray has
>them beat!  Alpha can make fast processors- at $2000, requiring over
>$500 in cache just to get near the Specmarks.  The entire point of
>RISC was to provide substantially better performance at the same
>price, or the same performance at a lower price.  This hasn't
>happened.  You blast the poster for stating that the Pentium has much

      Compare Alpha 21164/400 to P6/200:

      SPECint95/fp95   12.3/14.1 (workstation config) to 8.09/6.75

      The Alpha is about 1.5x faster in integer, and 2x faster in
      FP;  The Alpha dissipates 25 W worst case vs. 35 W for P6.

      The Alpha die is about the same size as the P6 die, 209 vs 197 mm2
      despite 1) the Intel process has much tighter metalization rules
      and 2) the Alpha has an integrated 96 KB L2 cache while the P6 L2
      cache has to be put on a separate die.

      The Alpha is made in a cheaper process (CMOS vs BICMOS, relaxed
      upper level processing rules) and has head room for frequency
      increases (500 MHz just announced) while Intel is scrambling to
      shrink P6/Klamath to 0.28 um for 266/300 MHz parts.  The Alpha
      die is very likely cheaper to manufacture than the P6 CPU die
      alone (i.e. not including the L2 cache die).  The Alpha is put
      into a plain CPGA while the P6 is packaged in a custom multi-chip
      module (MCM) with CPU die, L2 die, and probably pullup resistors
      for the GTL CPU <-> L2 bus.

      The packaged Alpha is less expensive than the P6 MCM to make and
      doesn't have to pull "bleeding edge" system level tricks like the
      P6's GTL system bus and 4-bit digitally tweaked power supply to
      achieve its performance.

      The Alpha vs P6 demonstrates RISC vs CISC quite nicely:

       - RISC is faster than CISC even though
             - the RISC design had far less NRE $ poured into it
             - the RISC chip is cooler
             - the RISC chip is cheaper to build (far cheaper in
               equivalent volumes)
             - the CISC chip was built in a faster process (bipolar
               transistors available, higher interconnect density)

      Don't let the $1500 Alpha price vs $700 P6 price fool you, the
      Alpha chip is less expensive to build.  The pricing reflects
      both the performance differences and vendor desire to match the
      market's current propensity to buy the product with the volumes
      the manufacturer can supply (i.e. how high can I price it to
      sell all the chips I can or want to make?)

      BTW, the new announced Alpha pricing structure is approx:
            $700 - 366 MHz, $1000 - 400 MHz and $1500 - 433 MHz
 

>higher performance than the PSX's CPU because he's ignoring the cost,
>and then act as if cost is irrelevant- choose a side, will you?
>

      The real interesting battle will be next year between the Klamath
      and 21164PC in the NT mid range and (ROTFL) the 21264 vs P7 at
