The cache was 2 MB 10 ns parts. Yest, other processors like R10000, 
Ultrasparc and PPC620 can also make good use of PBSRAM with their 128-bit 
cache buses, but Pentium Pro, Alpha's main target in NT market, is stuck 
with fixed 256K or 512K (though full-speed) 64-bit wide cache. Next 
year's Klamath MMX PPro will have half-speed external cache, and Intel 
didn;t confirm to me whether they will widen the 2nd cache bus to 128 
bits - it seems they won't from their AGP presentation on the Web site: 
it quotes some early '97 233 MHz MMX PPro cache bandwidth timings out of 
which you can see that is it half the bandwidth of existing PPro...

So, as 21164-500 can have a 125 MHz 3rd cache bus with 2 GB/s bandwidth, 
it is much better than the Klamath could ever have (even 250 MHz Klamath 
would only have half that bandwidth, and probably only up to 1 MB cache 
support).

Again, the 8-way 21264 will be unveiled in October at Microprocessor 
Forum: samples 1Q'97, volumes late 2Q'97 - it should scale to 600 MHz 
during 1997: your dearest Merced P7 would really need a miracle to appear 
before 4Q'98, as far as all trade rag reports say, and I don't think its 
8 planned exec units would match what 21264 offers (of course, they got 
enough time till then to revise the design couple more times). Also, 
Alpha is improving to solve the problem of character manipulation: 
remeber those new instructions in 21164A (Office97 should use them?)

Finally, Alpha is not just Digital anymore:  now there are two more chip 
sources who are primarily in for NT market: Mitsubishi (the $500 21164PC 
coming out in 4Q), and Samsung who'll also produce the 21264 next year. 
And, you also got system vendors like DeskStation (www.dti.com) who have 
their own Alpha chipsets, often beating DEC in NT product launches for 
half a year or so - visit this Siggraph show in August to see what the 
ultimate NT desktop is.

All this is not a matter of hating Intel: we simply need a stable 
alternative CPU architecture running NT well that will provide:
- compelling performance advantage for power users to switch (PoorerPC 
doesn't have this clear even with 604e-225)
- hdw compatibility
- low pricing potential if going into volumes
- multiple chip sources
Alpha fits all these now.

N Novakovic
nova@pacific.net.sg



