To anyone who can be of assistance:

I am currently have a rather nasty time of porting a piece of VAX Fortran
code to SPARC Fortran on a SUN Sparcstation.  The piece of code in question
opens a file, writes to it, and closes the file.  When this program is
run on a VAX, after its completion, a procedure is invoked which copies the
data file (created during the run of the Fortran program) via RCP from one
VAX disk to another VAX disk.  Another VAX Fortran program is then run,
which uses the newly created data file as input.  

When I run the UNIX version of the Fortran program, the data file is
created.  Upon terminating, I then have a C-shell script on the UNIX
system, which performs the RCP of the data file to the VAX disk.  The
other VAX Fortran program is then run, which uses this data file as
input.  However, the VAX program chokes on the file and traces back with
a stack dump.

I am hoping to modify the UNIX Fortran code to properly create the file
in the first place, such that, when the file is RCP'd to the VAX disk,
the file has the proper form to serve as input to the VAX Fortran program.

Here is the File I/O code in the original VAX FORTRAN:
______________________________________________________


OPEN STATEMENT:
______________

          OPEN(UNIT=IUNIT,FILE=NAME.TEXT,RECL=16300,STATUS='NEW',
     1         ERR=30,RECORDTYPE='VARIABLE',IOSTAT=IOERROR)

LATER ON, A WRITE STATEMENT:
___________________________

C     If the buffer for the Guide Star File is full then re-calculate
C     the Current_Place pointer, write that buffer to the Guide Star File
C     and if the Overflow Buffer was used then move that data to the
C     main buffer
C
          IF ((CURRENT_PLACE + I) .GE. 16300) THEN
            CURRENT_PLACE = (COUNT+CURRENT_PLACE) - 16300
            WRITE(IUNIT,10,ERR=40,IOSTAT=IOERROR)MAIN_BUFFER  <-------- WRITE
            MAIN_BUFFER = ' '
            IF (USE_AUX) THEN
              MAIN_BUFFER(1:CURRENT_PLACE) = AUX_BUFFER(1:CURRENT_PLACE)
              AUX_BUFFER = ' '
            END IF


LATER ON, THE FILE IS CLOSED:
____________________________

C     If a request has been made to close the Guide Star File and
C     the main buffer is empty then just close the file
C
        IF ((GS_REQ_DATA.CLOSE_REQUEST_FIL .EQ. '1') .AND. (MAIN_BUFFER .EQ.
        1        BLANK_REC)) THEN
          CLOSE (UNIT=IUNIT,STATUS='SAVE',ERR=50,IOSTAT=IOERROR)
        END IF


OR ELSE, MORE DATA IS WRITTEN USING "FUNKY" FORMAT STATEMENT 15 AND IS CLOSED:
______________________________________________________________________________

C
C     If a request has been made to close the Guide Star File and
C     the main buffer is not empty then write that last buffer to
C     the Guide Star File and then close the file
C

        IF ((GS_REQ_DATA.CLOSE_REQUEST_FIL .EQ. '1') .AND. (MAIN_BUFFER .NE.
        1        BLANK_REC)) THEN
          WRITE(IUNIT,15,ERR=40,IOSTAT=IOERROR) MAIN_BUFFER(1:CURRENT_PLACE)
          CLOSE(UNIT=IUNIT,STATUS='SAVE',ERR=50,IOSTAT=IOERROR)
        ENDIF

FORMAT STATEMENTS:
__________________

 10     FORMAT (A16300)
 15     FORMAT(<CURRENT_PLACE>A)

______________________________________________________________

I compiled this subroutine with SPARC Fortran, and the compiler complained that
RECORDTYPE='VARIABLE' is not supported, so I eliminated it from the open
statement.  The routine then compiled.  When I eventually ran the program,
I was told, via a screen message created by the Fortran compiler, that because
the file was opened for sequential access, the RECL=16300 qualifier is being
ignored.  So, I also eliminated the RECL=16300 part of the open statement:

NEW OPEN STATEMENT:
__________________

          OPEN(UNIT=IUNIT,FILE=NAME.TEXT,STATUS='NEW',ERR=30,IOSTAT=IOERROR)


___________________________

When I ran the VAX version of the program, followed by the UNIX version, I
then copied the newly created data files (2, 1 from VAX run, 1 from UNIX run)
onto a VAX disk.  I then did a DIRECTORY/FULL on both files, and this is what
I got:

