Take a look at the links to other computer-related web pages from my
web page; one of them points to J. Lothian's PDP-11/750 FAQ.

>first inclination is to get it running again since I collect old computers 
>and a running 
>VAX would be a great addition to my collection.   The down side is there 
>are no manuals 
>and it has not been turned on in six years. 

Before you power it up, please disconnect the PSU from everything
except the mains, and test the output voltages.  Also, it's very
important to make sure that nothing's gotten shifed around, or fallen
in anywhere, which can short anything out.  Years ago, I brought a
computer home, which had been in working condition, and when I powered
it up, sparks flew - it turned out that someone had left a piece of
loose solder in the chasis which shifted when the machine was moved.

There have been threads on occasion in PDP-11 newsgroups about what to
do before powering up a machine which hasn't been used in a long time.

Other things that you might want to check are the fans (do they spin
freely), condition of wiring, dust, spiders (dead or alive - could
possibly short something out), etc.  Hopefully some others,
particularly those familiar with this machine, will point out some
more things along these lines.

>This unit was owned by a company that manufactures RF parts (clocks, 
>filters, Etc.) and 
>they used it and a twin for product design and modeling in the late 80's.  

Do you have any idea as to what software may have come with it?
There's some nice circuit simulation software from back then which
runs on VAXen, and can used in conjunction with software running
on other systems.

>Power supply, fan, Etc.
>Battery (supposed to power the memory during a power wink, I think this 
>one is probably 
>dead)

I think that I'd definitely replace it, and at least remove it before it
leaks any acid or causes any corrosion.

Best of luck in getting this system working again.  

