In article <31DBE381.19AC@ilt.fhg.de>, Bruno Weikl <weikl@ilt.fhg.de> writes:
> Hi all,
> 
> we want to buy a Digital StorageWorks 310 array and are looking for alternatives to 
> expensive RZ29 drives. Digital uses Seagate and Quantum drives for the RZ29, so it 
> should be possible to buy from an alternate source.

I had a long conversation with several storage systems folks at DECUS in
St. Louis about just this question, for exactly the same reason.  They
noted that when the Digital qualification team takes a SCSI disk and puts
it through the paces, they almost invariably end up finding some problems
with the disk.  The problems may be related to volume of data (since the
Alphas often push very hard on the envelope) or to specifics of vibration
and physical factors inside of the Storageworks cabinet.   Very often,
there are problems of data corruption.  

In particular, there were several changes in the RZ29B microcode which
Digital mandated that Seagate put in before they would buy (and resell)
them.  Some of those changes may have propagated back to the other
Seagates; some may not.  Some changes may break other manufacturer's
controllers/cabinets.  Some drives in the channel may have new firmware and
some may not. 

The bottom line is that Digital will warrant that the drives will work if
you buy their drives.  If you don't, and they don't work, then it's your
problem.  Given the cost of people time, the cost of downtime, and the cost
differential in the drives, my company elected to bite the bullet and buy
Digital drives.  We plan to push them as hard as we can, and we want to
make sure that we're not going to look stupid because we saved a few
hundred dollars per drive.  

If you're going to stick them on a Sun or something else or a 125 MHz Alpha
or whatever, you may not feel that you're going to push the drives that
hard and you may want to get 3rd party disks.   

