> Alphas often push very hard on the envelope) or to specifics of vibration
> and physical factors inside of the Storageworks cabinet.   Very often,
> there are problems of data corruption.
[...etc., deleted...]

        Thanks for that review, Joel.

        It turns out we had  a  group from StorageWorks give a presenta-
    tion  here  at  SLAC  within the two week.  My take on  it  is  just
    slightly different.

        A) As Joel  said,  Digital  puts  these drives through their
           paces  and  winds  up  having  all  custom  (to  Digital)
           firmware.  This  way,  Digital  can  guarantee  that  the
           drives  will  work with their RAID controllers, etc.  The
           party line is  that  Digital  will  charge a 15% premium,
           relative to the same OEM drive without their firmware, as
           a   fair  mark-up  for  the  qualification  process   and
           supported firmware.

        B) The firmware is  _common_  to all operating systems: VMS,
           Digital UNIX, IBM, Sun, SGI, NT, etc.  This means that if
           you  buy  a drive from Digital for use on one  O/S,  then
           later want to use it on another, it _will_ work  with  no
           change to the firmware.

        C) They also  intimated  that  if  you  were  just  going to
           through  the  drives on a SCSI, e.g., as JBOD, that  it's
           probably not worth getting  the  Digital  drives.   (Yes,
           they  said  that...more  than  once!  :-) Just go get the
           Seagate or Quantum equivalent.

        D) OTOH, they said that _if_ you are buying any kind of RAID
           system, whether mirroring, striping, RAID 3, RAID 5, or a
           combination, that they recommended  buying the disks from
           the  RAID system vendor since RAID makes special  demands
           on the drives and their firmware, and you don't  want  to
           be  pointing  fingers or opening yourself to data loss or
           corruption.  The RAID vendor knows  what disks & firmware
           will  work with their systems.  (Seems like sound  advice
           to me.)

        D) The vibration issues are especially important for the new
           3 1/2" 9GB drives.  They  felt these drives would require
           more  rigid support, as afforded by the SW300 cabinet for
           example.


        To sum up, if you're just looking  to  get a bunch of disks in a
    convenient enclosure, and if your O/S doesn't make heavy I/O demands
    on the disk subsystem, then go ahead and buy from Seagate or Quantum
    directly.   However,  if you expect to do any kind of RAID, pay  the
    premium up-front to Digital and have some confidence that your  data
    will  be  safe (and that they'll support you should you unearth some
    problem).

        -Ken
