(Malcolm Dunnett) wrote:

> In article <1996Jul5.150755.1@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu>,
>    fairfield@sldb4.slac.stanford.edu writes:
> > 
> >         A) As Joel  said,  Digital  puts  these drives through their
> >            paces  and  winds  up  having  all  custom  (to  Digital)
> >            firmware.  This  way,  Digital  can  guarantee  that  the
> >            drives  will  work with their RAID controllers, etc.  The
> >            party line is  that  Digital  will  charge a 15% premium,
> >            relative to the same OEM drive without their firmware, as
> >            a   fair  mark-up  for  the  qualification  process   and
> >            supported firmware.
> > 
> 
>     a 15% premium is perfectly reasonable and I would gladly pay it
> to get genuine Digital drives and the peace of mind that buys. Unfortunately
> my experience is that Digitals premium is more like 75% to 100% over that
> of the competition (eg the DECDirect price for an RZ29B drive is around
> $2800 CDN, compared to $1400-1600 for a Seagate or Quantum drive of
> similar size). If Digital thinks its drives are priced at 15% above the
> going "commodity" price they are, IMHO, seriously out of touch with reality.

The premium you pay depends on the way you purchase goods.  We've gone to
tender quite a few times over the last few years, and every time Digital
has managed to come out ahead (usually a Digital reseller, not Digital
themselves).  We did notice that the quoted price is always considerably
lower than DECdirect, and you're not doing yourselves any favours at all
buying at the DECdirect price.  There are many, many resellers that are
quite willing to sell you the same drives at lower cost.  Malcolm, if you
want the names of a couple in Victoria or Vancouver, feel free to e-mail
me.  

We've also found that Canadian resellers typically underbid US resellers,
and frequently by quite a large margin.  Our last order (in a different
group than mine) went to a US reseller (Digital Creations I think), and
that's a first for us.  We've usually found that the resellers can ship
drives very quickly, sometimes as little in a few days.

Our only VMScluster experience with 3rd party drives was several years
ago.  30 Seagate drives showed up, were tested, and 30 went back to the
vendor.  They were replaced with Digital DSP3210 drives back then, and
we've been fine ever since.  Not only did the vendor (Open Storage
Solutions) ship us 30 Digital drives, he also shipped us cold spares in
case one failed so that we could be back up and running quicker with the
basic return to depot warranty.
OSS normally warrants 48 hours turnaround on RTD warranty, but OSS did
even better.  

I haven't found it a problem getting competitive quotes on Digital
drives.  You just have to find some vendors that are willing to work with
you a little (and ignore DECdirect!).  Our VMScluster is running on
DSP3210, RZ28, RZ26, and RZ28B drives, plus some older SI-511 SDI disks
for page/swap.  Nice and quick, and very, very reliable now on the CMD
Hawk and Digital HSJ controllers.

-- 

