Tim Dantzie (zotim@unl.ac.uk) wrote:

> I'm trying to establish a real understanding of performance figures as
> indicated in the various computing press available. For example, VAX
> systems are/were measured in VUPS (Vax Units of Processing). The terms now
> being used are 'Specint95, Specfp95, TPC-C, tpmC, MHz etc'. Can anyone
> explain them clearly? Understanding Mhz is straightforward but
> understanding what the rest mean and their implications to me and making
> decisions in hardware purchasing are not. Is there a good publication
> explaining/discussing these? If so, can you point me in the right
> direction?

During the late 1980s, most of the major processor manufacturers were able to
agree on a common set of CPU benchmarks.  The industry committee that was
formed to do the work (SPEC) issued their first set of benchmarks in 1992, and
a new suite in 1995.  Basically, a few benchmark programs are compiled and
run on the CPU; the results are then averaged and published.  

The reason that a standardized benchmark is required, as opposed to comparing
clock speeds, is that some processors, by virtue of design, do more work in
a single cycle than others.  This is true even within processor families, as
the design is improved.

TPC is a cross-platform standardized benchmark for databases.  It measures
both performance and the cost of performance.  Since whole system performance
is critical in database applications, it also portends to measure I/O and
memory system performance, to a certain extent.

VUPS is a measure of processor performance from the old VAX days.  One VUPS
was supposaidly the performance of a VAX-11/780.  By a happy coincidence, the
SPECint92 benchmark also finds the 11/780 at unity.

The definitive study of processor design is Hennesey and Patterson's text,
which is now in its second edition.  Both SPEC and TPC have web sites with
the officially sanctioned benchmark results.


