Timothy Morgan <tmorgan@llanelli.me.lsu.edu> writes:

>I am trying to run Open3D on a DEC Alpha 3000 Model 400 with the HX
>graphics option.  I know that I will not be able to use the 3D tools of
>OpenGL and PEX, but can the 2D tools from OpenGL be used on a machine
>with only the HX graphics option.  Thanks for anyone's input.

OpenGL is a 3D protocol; even the glVertex2f commands cause the
system to go through the full 3D pipeline. There is no way that Digital
could produce a 2D only OpenGL -- to use the name OpenGL we must
provide a full implementation.

You can try the software-only version of the OpenGL renderer
in the latest Open3D kit (Open3D Version 3.3 for Digital UNIX Version 3.2)
but it is very slow and not supported on the HX. You also need to 
get a license for Open3D.

If you are doing OpenGL you should really should get a more recent 
graphics board than the HX -- which came out maybe 10 years ago.

Finally, you can try the OpenGL-like API called Mesa, which is a free
implementation that works on X11 displays. I don't know what kind
of performance you might see. For information, see:

	http://www.ssec.wisc.edu/~brianp/Mesa.html

-- 
