From: shannon@world.std.com (Terry Shannon)
Subject: Re: Why is DEC having financial problems?
Date: 1996/07/04
Message-ID: <shannon-0407961107550001@10.0.2.15>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 163680540
sender: news@world.std.com (Mr Usenet Himself)
references: <4rgfhs$761@amenti.rutgers.edu>
organization: Shannon Knows DEC
newsgroups: comp.org.decus,comp.sys.dec


In article <4rgfhs$761@amenti.rutgers.edu>, estock@amenti.rutgers.edu
(Richard Estock) wrote:

> Wednesday's (7/3/96) NY Times capsulized DEC's problems as:
> 
> "But the Biggest challenge remains the company's personal computer
> business, which as been struggling for 18 months.  Mr. Palmer
> [DEC's President] said yesterday that Digital had been caught short
> earlier this year when it sold far too many machines to resellers,
> just as PC prices dropped steeply.  ...
> 
> "The real issue is getting the right amount of stuff in the channel,"
> he said, alluding to independent resellers who buy machines from
> Digital and sell them to customers.'" ....
> 
> The above statements make little sense to me.  How it is that DEC was
> "caught short" by selling "too many machines" BEFORE the prices
> dropped?
> 

DEC sold the PCs in question to intermediaries (resellers), not to end
users. And the products were very aggressively pushed into the channels:
until early this year, DEC's focus was on gaining PC market share. This
maniacal focus caused the firm to neglect a key business metric--making a
profit.

As PC prices began to decline, DEC was forced to compensate its resellers
(who of course did not want to get stuck holding the bag). The value of
the products in the channels began to melt like the proverbial ice cube,
and DEC ended up taking a bath.

Ironically, Bruce Claflin's decision to exit the consumer PC market
vindicates Bob Palmer. During a conversation a couple of years, he
indicated that he had strong reservations about entering the consumer
business. Turns out that these misgivings were well-founded.

I don't believe DEC will completely exit the PC market--in fact, the
firm's WNT-centric Windows-based Enterprise Computing initiative hinges on
the availability of Intel desktops and servers--but it wouldn't surprise
me to see a whole lotta outsourcing going on.

The PCBU Blues took about $50M out of DEC's 3FQ96 profit, and are likely
to do nearly twice as much damage in 4FQ96 when the numbers come out at
the end of this month. 4FQ96 profit is likely to be in the neighborhood of
$75M or so. For the year, profit will be under $400M, which of course is
more than offset by the $475M restructuring charge the firm took in 4FQ96.

The bulk of the impending layoffs--which will occur over the coming six to
twelve months--will likely impact the SBU and the former ABU. Current
plans are to cut the PCBU by roughly 700 positions. There is also likely
to be some business unit consolidation; the elimination of the CPBU
wouldn't surprise me.

-- 
Terry C. Shannon, Editor & Publisher, Shannon Knows DEC
