In article <4r965l$inj@news.psc.edu>, Nigel Goddard  <ngoddard@psc.edu> wrote:
>static void F(x)
>     float x;
>{
>  (void) printf("%f\n", x);
>  G(&x);
>}

The "float" you have there is really a double, according to the promotion
rules. So you're passing a pointer to a double to a function which expects
a pointer to a float.

Try adding the following line:

	printf("%d %d %d\n", sizeof x, sizeof(float), sizeof(double));

It shows 8 4 8.



