In <4r965l$inj@news.psc.edu> Nigel Goddard <ngoddard@psc.edu> writes:

>Is this one known?  Is there a fix?

Yes and yes.

>Briefly, code taking the address of a function parameter declared float 
>in the old style is not compiled correctly.

A function parameter declared float in an old style function definition
has the type double.

>Here is a sample program
>
>#include <stdio.h>
>
>static void G(px)
>     float *px;
>{
>  (void) printf("%f\n", *px);
>}
>
>static void F(x)
>     float x;

The compiler is required to treat this declaration as

      double x;

>{
>  (void) printf("%f\n", x);
>  G(&x);

Now you pass a pointer to double to a function expecting a pointer to
float.  All the bets are off.

>}
>
>int main ()
>{
>  F(0.2);

The type of the constant 0.2 is double, but this is OK, because the
function F actually expects a double argument.

>  return(0);
>}

Since the bug is in your code, _you_ have to fix it.  Either by switching
to ANSI prototypes or by declaring the argument of the function G as

    double *px;

Dan
